\par\par \b \uc2 Blame the U.S. for the Weaker Chinese  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  -- Streetwise\b0\par\par\uc2 By James Mackintosh \par \uc2 855 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Oktober 2018\par 19:34\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 The U.S. is due to decide this week whether to designate China a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator. While it's not expected to apply the label on this go around, the yuan has fallen more than 9% against the dollar since it last considered the move in April.\par \par \uc2 Tensions between the two nations have been building since then. It is tempting to think that China must be actively pushing its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  down in an attempt to offset the effect of the 10% tariffs being imposed on its exports to the U.S. by President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  . It is also tempting to want to punish China for what Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  earlier this month called "\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation" in a long list of Chinese policies in breach of "free and fair" trade. Both are mistaken.\par \par \uc2 Arguably the yuan should have weakened more. It is being pushed down by a slowing economy and easier monetary policy, at the same time that the dollar is being pushed up by the supercharged U.S. economy and higher interest rates. U.S. tariffs should lead to a weaker yuan and stronger dollar, too, even in a free market.\par \par \uc2 To simplify: "When bad things happen to a country, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  goes down," says \uc2 Alan Ruskin\uc2  , global co-head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 The effects of the strong dollar are obvious everywhere, with major emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  --Mexico's peso aside -- all falling even more than the yuan against the greenback this year. The yuan is down a barely noticeable 2.3% against the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the next-largest economy, the euro. And adjusted for China's higher consumer price inflation, it is down only 2.6% against its global trading partners, according to \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  . The true issue this year is the strong dollar, not the weak yuan.\par \par \uc2 If anything, China has been preventing its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from falling further. \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has recently pushed up the cost of borrowing for offshore speculators wanting to bet against the yuan, too, even as it loosened restrictions on bank lending domestically. And as \uc2 Nicholas Lardy\uc2  , senior fellow at the \uc2 Peterson Institute for International Economics\uc2  says, it has mostly been setting its daily fix -- where trade starts each day -- slightly stronger than the previous night's close.\par \par \uc2 "The Chinese have been doing quite a bit to slow down the rate of depreciation," Mr. Lardy said.\par \par \uc2 Why would China want to keep its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stronger? In the long run because it has been trying to encourage more consumption, which is helped by having a stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Lots of exports are nice to have, but workers want cheap imports and foreign travel, too.\par \par \uc2 In the short run the incentive is to avoid panic. In 2015 China made a mistake by suddenly devaluing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , prompting speculators to pile in to bet against the yuan -- and domestic companies to unwind the huge bets they had made on the yuan rising. The fall began to become self-fulfilling, and China burned through half a trillion dollars of reserves trying to halt the drop before tighter capital controls finally led to a rebound.\par \par \uc2 The lesson for China was to avoid drama in the exchange rate, keeping the decline in check and restricting outflows of money. So far it has worked, with \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves down only $74 billion, or 2%, from January's peak.\par \par \uc2 This is manipulation, but not the sort that the U.S. worries about. Worse, if \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  did as the U.S. wants and decided to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to push the yuan up, it would have perverse and unwelcome effects on the U.S. bond market.\par \par \uc2 To understand this, consider how China used to hold its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  down by building up \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves -- something that was obviously manipulation. When dollars flowed into the country, either from investment or from its trade surplus, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  recycled them back into U.S. Treasurys rather than let the yuan rise. This made China's exports more competitive until its wages rose, and, according to former Federal Reserve Chairman \uc2 Ben Bernanke\uc2  's " global savings glut" thesis, held down U.S. interest rates.\par \par \uc2 If \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  were to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to strengthen its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  now, it would mean selling U.S. Treasurys, or at least not rolling over maturing holdings, and selling the dollars for yuan. With markets already on high alert over rising bond yields, adding to the selling pressure on Treasurys wouldn't be welcome.\par \par \uc2 Finally, the entire U.S. approach is odd. The Treasury's tests label countries as \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulators only if they are deliberately weakening their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , not if they are deliberately strengthening them. If we cared about free markets, as Mr. Pence claimed, both should be seen as equally bad.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Pence is right that there is no proper free market in the yuan. But if there was, it would most likely be falling even faster than it has been.\par \par \uc2 Write to James Mackintosh at James.Mackintosh@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 October 15, 2018 13:34 ET (17:34 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020181015eeaf002b4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
