\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Asia shares plummet after Wall St rout; Shanghai at near 4-year lows\b0\par\par\uc2 926 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Oktober 2018\par 06:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2018 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * Nikkei sheds 4.3 pct, Asia ex-Japan at lowest since March 2017               \par \par \uc2 * Shanghai index hits lowest level since late 2014               \par \par \uc2 * Culprits include high bond yields, Sino-US trade tensions               \par \par \uc2 * Trump calls Fed "crazy" for raising interest rates               \par \par \uc2 * Yen and euro in demand as safe harbours, dollar mixed               \par \par \uc2 By Wayne Cole and Andrew Galbraith               \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY/SHANGHAI, Oct 11 (Reuters) - Asian share markets sank in a sea of red on Thursday after Wall Street suffered its worst drubbing in eight months, a conflagration of wealth that could threaten business confidence and investment across the globe.               \par \par \uc2 "Equity markets are locked in a sharp sell-off, with concern around how far yields will rise, warnings from the \uc2 IMF\uc2  about financial stability risks and continued trade tension all driving uncertainty," summed up analysts at ANZ.               \par \par \uc2 The global plunge erased hundreds of billions of dollars of wealth. The head of the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  said stock market valuations have been "extremely high".               \par \par \uc2 MSCI's broadest index of Asia-Pacific shares outside Japan plummeted 3.9 percent to its lowest since March 2017.               \par \par \uc2 Japan's Nikkei fell 4.4 percent, the steepest daily drop since March, while the broader TOPIX lost around $230 billion in market value.               \par \par \uc2 Shanghai shares dropped 4.3 percent, on track for their worst day since February 2016, to their lowest level since late 2014, while China blue chips slid 4 percent.               \par \par \uc2 Shares in Taiwan were among the region's worst-hit, with the broader index losing 6.2 percent.               \par \par \uc2 "We can't see where the bottom point will be," said Chien Bor-yi, an analyst at Taipei-based Cathay Futures Consultant.               \par \par \uc2 On Wall Street, the S&P500's sharpest one-day fall since February wiped out around $850 billion of wealth as technology shares tumbled on fears of slowing demand.               \par \par \uc2 The S&P 500 ended Wednesday with a loss of 3.29 percent and the Nasdaq Composite 4.08 percent, while the Dow shed 2.2 percent.               \par \par \uc2 The blood letting was bad enough to attract the attention of U.S. President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who pointed an accusing finger at the Fed for raising interest rates.               \par \par \uc2 "I really disagree with what the Fed is doing," Trump told reporters before a political rally in Pennsylvania. "I think the Fed has gone crazy."               \par \par \uc2 It was hawkish commentary from Fed policymakers that triggered the sudden sell off in Treasuries last week and sent long-term yields to their highest in seven years.               \par \par \uc2 The surge made stocks look less attractive compared to bonds while also threatening to curb economic activity and profits.                              \par \par \uc2 YUAN A FLASHPOINT               \par \par \uc2 The shift in yields is also sucking funds out of emerging markets, putting particular pressure on the Chinese yuan as Beijing fights a protracted trade battle with the United States.               \par \par \uc2 On Thursday, the president of the World Bank said he is very concerned about trade tensions and warned of a "clear" global economic slowdown if tariff threats escalate.               \par \par \uc2 China has suspended approvals for an overseas investment product in Shanghai and has asked license holders such as JPMorgan Asset Management and Aberdeen Standard Investments to be "low profile" in marketing it, as concerns rise in Beijing over possible outflow pressures.               \par \par \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been allowing the yuan to gradually decline, breaking the psychological 6.9000 barrier and leading speculators to push the dollar up to 6.9388 at 0342 GMT.               \par \par \uc2 The onshore yuan was trading at 6.9309 per dollar at 0350 GMT, 69 pips weaker than the onshore close of 6.9240 Wednesday.               \par \par \uc2 China's move has forced other emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken to stay competitive, and drawn the ire of the United States which sees it as an unfair devaluation.               \par \par \uc2 "The yuan has already weakened significantly, to offset the tariffs announced so far," said \uc2 Alan Ruskin\uc2 , Deutsche's global head of G10 \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy. "Further weakness could exacerbate concerns of a self-fulfilling flight of capital, and a loss of control."               \par \par \uc2 There was also a danger for the U.S. if Beijing had to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily to support the yuan.               \par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  buying yuan and selling dollars would likely entail some selling of U.S. Treasuries at a point where the market is showing some vulnerability, and could be very vulnerable to signs of China liquidation," added Ruskin.               \par \par \uc2 The dollar was already losing ground to both the yen and the euro, as investors favoured \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of countries that boasted large current account surpluses.               \par \par \uc2 The euro pushed up to $1.1565 and away from a low of $1.1429 early in the week. The dollar lapsed to 112.17 yen , down 0.1 percent and a telling retreat from last week's 114.54 peak.               \par \par \uc2 That left the dollar at 95.207 against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .               \par \par \uc2 In commodity markets, gold struggled to get any safety bid and edged down to $1,193.40. Oil prices skidded in line with U.S. equity markets, even though energy traders worried about shrinking Iranian supply from U.S. sanctions and kept an eye on Hurricane Michael, which closed some U.S. Gulf of Mexico oil output. Brent crude fell 1.9 percent to $81.51 a barrel, while  U.S. crude dropped 1.7 percent to $71.93.               \par \par \uc2 On Thursday, the head of the \uc2 IMF\uc2 , \uc2 Christine Lagarde\uc2 , warned that the global economy is "probably not strong enough" and that with global public debt an all-time high, emerging markets were at risk of capital outflows.                              \par \par \uc2 Released: 2018-10-11T05:30:59.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020181011eeab00g7d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
