\par\par \b \uc2 Yuan eases to 1-mth low, set for longest monthly losing streak since 1994\b0\par\par\uc2 825 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 September 2018\par 06:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2018 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     SHANGHAI, Sept 28 (Reuters) - China's yuan fell to a\par one-month low against the dollar on Friday, with escalating\par Sino-U.S. trade tensions putting the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on course for its\par longest monthly losing streak on record.\par     If the yuan finishes the late night session at the midday\par level, it will have lost 0.76 percent against the dollar for the\par month, the sixth straight monthly loss -- the longest such\par streak since the exchange rate was unified in 1994.\par     Prior to the market opening on Friday, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 People's Bank of\par China (PBOC) \f28 \uc2 \f2 set the midpoint rate at 6.8792 per\par dollar, the weakest level since Aug.17, 150 pips or 0.2 weaker\par than the previous fix of 6.8642.\par     In the spot market, the yuan opened at 6.8874 per\par dollar and fell to a low of 6.8910 at one point, the weakest\par level since Aug.24, before paring losses.\par     By midday, it was changing hands at 6.8830, 60 pips firmer\par than the previous late session close.\par     But volumes were thin with few participants willing to stake\par out fresh positions. Friday is the last trading day before the\par week-long National Day holiday that begins on Oct. 1.\par     Many traders expect the yuan to swing in a wide range of 6.8\par to 6.9 per dollar in the near term but with a downward bias as\par neither Washington nor Beijing appears willing to compromise on\par trade any time soon.\par     Economists also expect further policy easing measures in\par coming months in China if domestic and external demand continues\par to cool, adding pressure on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     However, a trader at a Chinese bank said the market was\par growing wary of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as spot yuan again nears 6.9, a\par level at which the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rolled out a slew of stabilising\par measures in August.\par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 Sheng Songcheng\f28 \uc2 \f2 , a policy adviser to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , told\par state-run Beijing News that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  still needs "appropriate\par guidance, management or even \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 " in the exchange rate.\par     "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's interest rate is in fact not fully market driven;\par some asset prices, such as housing prices, also need\par regulations," Sheng was quoted as saying. \par     "And it is obviously unrealistic to require the more\par sensitive exchange rate to realize a fully free float now. It\par should be a long-term target."\par     Gao Qi, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at Scotiabank in Singapore, said in a\par client note on Friday: "The \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  is expected to step in to curb\par one-way speculation if needed."\par     But he added that foreign portfolio inflows into Chinese\par stocks and bonds should boost the yuan over the medium to long\par term.\par     Global index provider FTSE Russell said on Thursday it will\par start including mainland Chinese shares in its major benchmarks\par from June next year, in a move that it expects will draw initial\par net inflows of $10 billion from passive investors.\par     In global markets, the dollar stood tall against its major\par trading partners on Friday, after data reinforced upbeat views\par about the U.S. economy and backed the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2 's signal\par for a steady course of rate increases over the next year.\par     The Thomson Reuters/HKEX Global CNH index, which\par tracks the offshore yuan against a basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on a\par daily basis, stood at 92.86, firmer than the previous day's\par 92.75. \par     The global dollar index rose to 94.945 from the\par previous close of 94.894. \par     The offshore yuan was trading 0.04 percent firmer\par than the onshore spot at 6.8803 per dollar. \par     Offshore one-year non-deliverable forwards contracts\par (NDFs), considered the best available proxy for\par forward-looking market expectations of the yuan's value, traded\par at 6.9615, 1.18 percent weaker than the midpoint.\par     One-year NDFs are settled against the midpoint, not the spot\par rate.\par     \par     The yuan market at 0407 GMT: \par     \par     ONSHORE SPOT:\par  Item               Current  Previous  Change\par  PBOC midpoint      6.8792   6.8642    -0.22%\par                                        \par  Spot yuan          6.883    6.889     0.09%\par                                        \par  Divergence from    0.06%              \par  midpoint*                             \par  Spot change YTD                       -5.46%\par  Spot change since 2005                20.25%\par  revaluation                           \par  \par     Key indexes:\par      \par  Item            Current     Previous  Change\par                                        \par  Thomson         92.86       92.75     0.1\par  Reuters/HKEX                          \par  CNH index                             \par  Dollar index    94.945      94.894    0.1\par  \par     \par     \par *Divergence of the dollar/yuan exchange rate. Negative number\par indicates that spot yuan is trading stronger than the midpoint.\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China (PBOC) \f28 \uc2 \f2 allows the exchange rate to\par 