\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Chinese PM calls for free trade in the face of escalating trade war with the US\b0\par\par\uc2 567 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 September 2018\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 Chinese Prime Minister \uc2 Li Keqiang\uc2  on Wednesday urged global support for free trade in the face of escalating trade disputes between Beijing and Washington sparked by China's technology policies. In a speech at the "Annual Meeting of New Champions," known as "Summer Davos," in the Chinese city of Tianjin, Li called for disputes to be resolved through consultations and called for multilateralism. US President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  announced on Tuesday customs duties on a total of $ 200 billion worth of imports from China. Beijing has said it will retaliate with "synchronized measures". "It is essential to uphold the basic principles of multilateralism and free trade," said the Chinese prime minister. "Unilateralism offers no viable solution," he said. \uc2 Li Keqiang\uc2 's words illustrate Beijing's efforts to position itself as a champion of international trade and multilateralism in the face of complaints from Washington or Brussels that China's industrial policies violate market-opening commitments.\par \par \uc2  The "Made in China 2025" plan, led by the Chinese state, aims to transform China into a technological power with capabilities in high added value sectors such as artificial intelligence, renewable energy, robotics and electric cars. The US condemns the forced transfer of technology by foreign companies in exchange for market access, subsidies to domestic firms, and regulatory obstacles that protect Chinese groups from foreign competition. Last July, Trump imposed $ 25 billion ($ 43 billion) worth of Chinese goods, and Beijing retaliated with taxes on the same amount of US imports. Trump now threatens to impose punitive duties on the remaining $ 267 billion of products imported from China to cover the country's entire US imports if Beijing fails to meet its demands to end "unfair" trade practices. Li indirectly acknowledged complaints about the regulation of global trade and affirmed China's support for its reform. "We believe that international rules must be upheld and at the same time improved so that they keep abreast of changes," he said. The Trump administration sees the \uc2 World Trade Organization\uc2  as antiquated and unable to deal with Beijing's "predatory" industrial policies. China agreed this year to work with the \uc2 European Union\uc2  on a reform of the agency. \uc2 Li Keqiang\uc2  also promised that Beijing will not devalue the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stimulate exports. The yuan's price has been falling against the dollar or the euro continuously since the start of trade disputes, suggesting that China is intentionally devaluing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . However, the \uc2 Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent weeks to halt the decline. "\b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  will never follow the path of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluation to boost exports," Li said, assuring the country will create conditions to keep the value of the yuan stable. The Chinese PM also promised to "improve the business environment" for foreign companies, reaffirming China's commitments to give equal treatment to foreign and domestic firms, a pledge made by the country when it joined the \uc2 WTO\uc2  in 2001, rather than enterprise groups and foreign governments say Beijing often does not comply. The \uc2 European Union\uc2  filed a lawsuit in the \uc2 WTO\uc2  last June because of the rules on technology licensing in China, which it considers to benefitto the detriment of foreign companies. "We will ensure that all companies, Chinese or foreign, as long as they are registered in China, will be treated equally," he said.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020180919ee9j001b9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
