\par\par \b \uc2 Chinese Banks Use Risky Tool To Woo Clients -- WSJ\b0\par\par\uc2 977 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 August 2018\par 08:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par B10\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 Structured deposits, which are tied to bets on certain assets, offer higher returns \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par By Shen Hong \f28 \par \par \uc2 This article is being republished as part of our daily reproduction of WSJ.com articles that also appeared in the U.S. print edition of The Wall Street Journal (August 22, 2018).\par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI -- Chinese banks are taking on new risks as they scramble to lure savers, turning a previously obscure line of business into a $1 trillion industry.\par \par \uc2 The explosion marks the latest effort by lenders to circumvent Beijing's campaign against financial risk, and to head off rising competition from the lightly regulated shadow-banking sector and upstart high-tech rivals such as \uc2 Ant Financial\uc2 . It also reflects an old struggle in a country where banks can't freely set their own interest rates.\par \par \uc2 Structured deposits offer higher returns than regular savings accounts and are tied to bets on assets from \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to gold. They have been around for years, but the sums outstanding have soared recently. In July they stood at a record 9.71 trillion yuan ($1.42 trillion), up 52% in a year, according to data provider Wind.\par \par \uc2 Banks aren't competing only with one another for funds, which they need to extend more loans; savers also are switching to more attractive options, such as high-yielding wealth-management products, and to money-market funds, of which the largest is Ant's Yu'E Bao. Growth in regular deposits fell to an annual pace of 8.5% in July from double-digit rates in previous years.\par \par \uc2 While lenders still sell their own wealth-management products, they are no longer allowed to guarantee buyers' principal or interest payments. But they can promise to protect a customer's outlay on a structured deposit.\par \par \uc2 "This is a new toy for Chinese banks to raise funds, especially after the authorities imposed fresh curbs on their ability to use wealth management products and other tools to expand business," said Yulia Wan, an analyst at Moody's Investors Service.\par \par \uc2 The investments are a form of structured product. In the U.S., this broader asset class enjoyed solid growth until the global financial crisis, when products issued by \uc2 Lehman Brothers\uc2  suffered steep losses. In 2013, the \uc2 Securities and Exchange Commission\uc2  introduced stricter disclosure requirements for structured notes, bonds that contain a derivative component.\par \par \uc2 Structured deposits now account for about 6% of all Chinese deposits, and are bought by both corporate and individual customers. They typically include maximum and minimum interest rates, with actual returns depending on the performance of a financial asset.\par \par \uc2 However, many of these structured deposits in China have structures arrangements in which the terms are "set so far off that investors can almost always get the advertised maximum return," said Ms. Wan at \uc2 Moody's\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In a 91-day product recently marketed by Weihai Blue Ocean Bank, the small lender from eastern Shandong province offered the equivalent of 5.28% a year if gold prices in London, now around $1,183 an ounce, stay between $300 and $2,200. If not, customers will receive 1.65%.\par \par \uc2 Wenjing Zhao, a Beijing-based public relations executive, said she bought the instrument because it looked safer than wealth management products, and mutual funds generally aren't doing well at the moment. Because "the targeted range of gold prices is so wide," Ms. Zhao said she was confident she will receive the maximum. "In the worst case, I'll still get 1.65%," she added.\par \par \uc2 Likewise, a similar product from \uc2 China Guangfa Bank\uc2 , a midsize institution in the southern city of Guangzhou, promises an annualized 4.55% unless the Hong Kong dollar breaks its official peg of 7.75 to 7.85 to the U.S. dollar and trades above 7 or below 9 per greenback.\par \par \uc2 In its marketing materials, Guangfa Bank says it will invest at least 20% of the proceeds in money-market instruments and up to 80% in bonds. Interest payments will come from unspecified derivatives tied to the U.S.-Hong Kong exchange rate, it says.\par \par \uc2 Officials at Guangfa Bank couldn't immediately be reached for comment. Weihai Blue Ocean Bank didn't respond to emailed questions from The Wall Street Journal.\par \par \uc2 Despite scrapping controls on deposit rates in 2015, \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has used unofficial \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  known as "window guidance" to cap banks' rates at 1.5 times benchmark levels. The official one-year rate is 1.5%.\par \par \uc2 "If there were full interest-rate liberalization, there wouldn't be a need for such products," said Ms. Wan, adding that superficially similar instruments in developed economies meet different needs.\par \par \uc2 Structured deposits naturally mean higher borrowing costs for lenders. And they could create other headaches, too. For example, if investments backing the products go sour, banks might be forced to pay up anyway.\par \par \uc2 While banks in places such as Singapore and Hong Kong also sell structured investments, they guarantee neither interest rates nor principal. Chinese equivalents guarantee the principal, and in practice many banks would probably feel obliged to ensure investors get the maximum interest payment too, bankers and analysts say.\par \par \uc2 "No matter how high the interest rates, we'll have to deliver because otherwise there will be huge reputational risk for us, and our customers will abandon us," said a China Citic Bank branch manager based in a city in northern China who declined to be named. Officials at \uc2 Citic Bank\uc2  couldn't immediately be reached for comment.\par \par \uc2 Robin Xing, an economist at Morgan Stanley, said this is the latest in a long line of initiatives to woo depositors. Before, he said, "banks had to hand out gifts such as rice cookers."\par \par \uc2 --Wei Zhou contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Shen Hong at hong.shen@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 22, 2018 02:32 ET (06:32 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020180822ee8m000g0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
