\par\par \b \uc2 Caution Despite Strong India Factory Output -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,213 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 August 2018\par 08:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0602 GMT - Although India's June industrial production jumped 7% last month, helping reinforce brightening prospects, some economists aren't too sure if growth this strong will last. \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  says India's recent move to increase tariffs on some textile imports would hurt local consumers and could also invite retaliatory action from the likes of China. That could be bad for Indian exporters, who have barely emerged from the financial troubles caused by the delays in tax refunds under GST. Rating firm \uc2 ICRA\uc2  adds that a slowdown in growth of coal, automobile and electricity production would also lead to some weakness in the months ahead. (anant.kala@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0556 GMT - India's capacity utilization is picking up, but not meaningfully enough to attract investments, says \uc2 Nomura\uc2 . The investment bank estimates that 2Q utilization was 72.3%, just above the past 2 years' lows and well below the roughly 78% seen from 2004-7. \uc2 Nomura\uc2  believes still-low utilization is one of the reasons why investment may take time to develop in India as initial bursts of demand will likely be met by sweating existing assets rather than building new capacity. (debiprasad.nayak@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0550 GMT - European bourses should kick off the week in negative territory as concerns over Turkey keep unnerving investors, \uc2 London Capital Group\uc2  says. "Traders were met with a sea of red as indices dived across the Asian session; investors shunned riskier assets overnight...; jitters surrounding the Turkish \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis were showing few signs of moving on," it says. Fears of contagion weigh on the markets. "An admission of concern by the \uc2 ECB\uc2  over the possible impact of contagion was unexpected and weighed heavily on demand for riskier assets," it says. France's \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  , Italy's \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  and Spain's \uc2 BBVA\uc2  are in focus due to their exposure to the country's assets and the Turkish lira, it adds.(pietro.lombardi@dowjones.com; @pietrolombard10)\par \par \uc2 0450 GMT - The New Zealand dollar, meanwhile, has been going through a bearish 2018 and the charts signal more weakness for it as well, says \uc2 NAB\uc2  . For example, monthly Bollinger bands have been breached and are diverging at a rate that \uc2 NAB\uc2  associates with a downtrend. Meanwhile, monthly MACD have confirmed such a move for the first time since 2014 and Fibonacci support at NZ$0.6675 was broken last week on a weekly closing basis. That implies a looming test of 2015's low of US$0.6130 in coming months, says \uc2 NAB\uc2  . The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is around US$0.6580. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0422 GMT - After sliding from 2011-5, the Aussie dollar rebounded from 2016 through early this year. The trendline was broken in April and a failed retest occurred in June. That highlights the ongoing downward move for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that helped push the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to levels last seen in the opening days of 2017, says \uc2 NAB\uc2  . It adds last week's break and close below trend-channel support at US$0.7330-50, a level which held for almost 2 months, is further confirmation of the downdraft. The Aussie is around US$0.7270. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0352 GMT - After Hong Kong GDP grew slower-than-expected 3.5% in 2Q, \uc2 OCBC\uc2  is among those anticipating further deceleration in 2H for the city's export-oriented economy. But beyond trade, which could hit Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and weighing on Hong Kong's tourism, it could also pressure local equities--and hence consumer sentiment. The investment bank says 2018's GDP growth could come in at 3.2%, versus last year's 3.8%, if trade tensions escalate. (chester.yung@wsj.com; @chester_yung)\par \par \uc2 0336 GMT - RBA Gov. Lowe has stressed over the last year that should wage growth not improve, the bank's inflation targets probably won't be achieved. That helps make Wednesday's 2Q wage reading notable. \uc2 RBC\uc2  see little reason for increases to deviate much from the recent quarterly pace of 0.5%. Just 2% annually is the kind of result that will have the chief central banker slumping back into his chair. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0311 GMT - The New Zealand and Australian dollars are respectively 6% and 2% below \uc2 NAB\uc2  's short-term fair-value estimates, suggesting growing discounts which reflect a more-uncertain global outlook. The investment bank expects to see another lurch down in the duo on any fresh tariffs of the scale proposed by the White House, meaning the kiwi falling below US$0.65 and the Aussie US$0.70 can't be ruled out. However, a de-escalation of trade tensions could generate modest recoveries in both \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0305 GMT - The upticks in Hong Kong mortgage rates "is in line with our view that financial conditions will be gradually tightened" there as local interest rates "eventually catch up with" those in the US, says \uc2 Nomura\uc2 . It helps reiterate the bank's call of GDP rising just 2.3% next year, versus 2018's projected 4%, "as we believe the burden of mortgage-debt servicing and a modest housing-market correction should lower consumption growth." (john.wu@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0255 GMT - The New Zealand economy is at a delicate juncture, says ANZ. Overall structural imbalances are not too bad, but home price/income ratios are well above sustainable levels, it contends. That as fiscal stimulus is the largest in years, the labor market is tight and consumers are still saying it's a great time to buy a major household item. Also, the country's terms of trade is nearly record highs. For ANZ, none of that takes away from the New Zealand remaining vulnerable or being able to shake the feeling that the external environment is as good as it gets. When businesses are feeling worried, the reaction to any bad news--in terms of shelving investment and hiring plans--could be swift. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0249 GMT - \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  's alleged \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market today helps send a message that rupiah stability remains its short-term priority, says a dealer with a foreign bank. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is suspected to have sold dollars at around IDR14,600 after the rupiah hit fresh 3-year lows. The pair has steadied around that level after getting to IDR14,648 low earlier in the day. \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  usually declines to confirm a specific \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (i-made.sentana@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0233 GMT - If \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's budget is fully implemented this year, the fiscal deficit would be 0.9% of GDP more than last year, or about CNY1.1 trillion ($161 billion), Standard Chartered estimates. Actual implementation in 1H pointed to a possible deficit undershooting, suggesting authorities are now under pressure to accelerate implementation through tax cuts and faster infrastructure spending, the investment bank says. That as the firm ticked up its 2018 GDP-growth target to 6.6% from 6.5% amid 1H's strength and what it calls the government's measures to support demand. (liyan.qi@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 13, 2018 02:02 ET (06:02 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020180813ee8d000ac\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
