 Crude oil ended lower. And gold settled lower as investors sought riskier targets. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par OPENING CALL \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Geopolitical concerns are growing, including retaliatory trade tariffs between the U.S. and China, U.S. planned sanctions on Russia and Turkey, and uncertainties regarding Brexit and Italian politics. ING says in the short term this could see investors "flock to the dollar," although its analysts are questioning "whether any \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is truly safe." This will leave the DXY dollar index anchored around 95, it says, keeping it close to July's one-year peak of 95.652. Dollar gains should keep EUR/USD below 1.16, it says. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EQUITIES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The S&P 500 edged lower, as the latest round of corporate earnings reports offered some support to a stock market reckoning with yet another trade volley.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The broad index struggled to gain traction, even after shares of several companies, including telecommunications firm \f28 \uc2 \f2 CenturyLink\f28 \uc2 \f2  and energy-drink maker \f28 \uc2 \f2 Monster Beverage\f28 \uc2 \f2  , rose following upbeat earnings results. After wobbling most of the day, the S&P 500 fell for a second consecutive session.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors said renewed trade tensions sapped some of the market's enthusiasm after China threatened Wednesday to slap new tariffs on various chemicals and medical equipment, among other goods. Beijing released the list of items as part of its promise to keep pace with the U.S.'s trade threats, which caused stocks to sputter Wednesday, snapping a four-session streak of gains for the S&P 500 and Dow Jones Industrial Average.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We're trying to deal with tariffs, but who knows what [President] Trump could do tomorrow, and we don't know which of our companies are going to be hurt by trade issues," said Charlie Smith, chief investment officer of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fort Pitt Capital Group\f28 \uc2 \f2  . "But the market is paying attention to whether tariffs are getting ramped up to the point it's hurting S&P earnings."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In Asia, stock markets were all over the map, with local factors dominating amid a lack of overseas trading cues.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Potential government \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in tech sent the sector higher in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , fueling gains of more than 2% for major indexes. That also helped Hong Kong.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A stronger \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  capped Japan's stocks. Japan's Nikkei edged lower 0.2% and Hong Kong's Hang Sang rose 0.9%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Philippines fell 0.4%, well off session lows, after a weak gross domestic product report and ahead of the imminent \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central-bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy statement.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The moves came as India's Sensex continued to hit more record highs. Indian shares were led by bank stocks. The S&P BSE Sensex closed at a fresh high of 38,024.37, up 0.4%, taking this year's gain to nearly 12%. Investors remain upbeat on bank stocks on hopes of lower bad loan provisions. \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 The New Zealand dollar saw a sharp move down against the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in response to a dovish message from its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Other G-10 \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  were more muted, including the U.S. dollar, which strengthened modestly after weekly jobless claims and producer-price data came in slightly below expectations.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Both reports showed small declines on the previous months. It indicates that there is a slight dip in demand. It is worth noting that last month's PPI and core PPI reports were the highest in years, so a slight pullback isn't a surprise. Tomorrow, traders will be keeping an eye on the inflation report," said \f28 \uc2 \f2 CMC Markets\f28 \uc2 \f2  analyst David Madden , in a note.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ICE U.S. Dollar Index was last up 0.4% at 95.444, putting it on track for a 0.3% gain for the week. The dollar's main rival, the euro was correspondingly weaker, dropping to $1.1545 from $1.1610, it is lowest level since May. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BONDS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. government\f28 \uc2 \f2  bonds gained after the Labor Department said producer prices remained flat in July, falling short of economist forecasts for them to rise.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yield benchmark 10-year Treasury note yield fell for a second consecutive day to 2.935% from 2.969% Wednesday. Yields fall as bond prices rise.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Yields fell after the Labor Department said the producer price index was flat in July, and that prices excluding the often-volatile food and energy categories prices rose 0.1%. Economists surveyed by The Wall Street Journal had expected a 0.2% increase in overall prices, a 0.2% rise for prices excluding food and energy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The index tracks cost pressures building in the pipeline for many businesses, which can be a precursor to broader inflation. Inflation poses a threat to the value of a bond's future interest and principal payments. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par COMMODITIES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Crude-oil prices edged lower, settling in negative territory for a second straight session.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 West Texas Intermediate crude oil for September delivery closed off 13 cents, or about 0.2%, at $66.81 a barrel.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Worries that demand has been weakening is a key theme that has recently weighed on crude prices. U.S. oil prices registered their steepest one-day drop in more than three weeks on Wednesday as the bullish narrative that had buoyed crude futures for the past two sessions - supply disruptions and strong demand - fizzled out.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors are now fretting that demand from the second-largest economy and biggest oil importer, China, may be hurt as the country faces economic slowdown and as trade clashes between President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Donald Trump\f28 \uc2 \f2  and Beijing show apparent signs of intensifying.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meanwhile, gold futures failed an attempt at a third straight gain, as the latest round of economic data on jobs and inflation offered little to dissuade investors from buying assets perceived as risky over haven gold.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 December gold lost $1.10, or less than 0.1%, to settle at $1,219.90 an ounce, halting three straight gains. The commodity has mostly traded within a narrow band and is hovering around the lows of the year. It is down about 1.1% for August so far and off about 7% in the year to date.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 September silver tacked on 3 cents, or 0.2%, to end at $15.462 an ounce. The white metal remains down nearly 10% so far in 2018, falling a sharp 4.2% in just the past 30 days. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TODAY'S HEADLINES \par For Uber and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Airbnb\f28 \uc2 \f2  , New York City Turns Foe \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Two measures in New York City could hamper Silicon Valley heavyweights \f28 \uc2 \f2 Uber Technologies\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Airbnb\f28 \uc2 \f2  as they prep for potential initial public offerings expected as soon as next year. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Samsung\f28 \uc2 \f2   Introduces Galaxy Note 9, a Smartphone Strikingly Like Its Predecessor \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Samsung\f28 \uc2 \f2  Galaxy Note 9 looks much like last year's Note 8, reflecting slowing smartphone innovation that has contributed to a historic dip in industry sales. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Sumner Redstone Trust Restricts Sale Options for \f28 \uc2 \f2 CBS\f28 \uc2 \f2  , \f28 \uc2 \f2 Viacom\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sumner Redstone doesn't want his heirs to have an easy time selling off his family's controlling stakes in media companies \f28 \uc2 \f2 Viacom\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 CBS\f28 \uc2 \f2  . And he put it in writing in his estate-planning documents. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Tribune  Terminates $3.9 Billion Merger With Rival Sinclair \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tribune Media terminated its merger agreement with rival TV station-owner \f28 \uc2 \f2 Sinclair Broadcast Group\f28 \uc2 \f2  and sued the company, alleging it failed to make sufficient efforts to get their $3.9 billion deal approved by regulators. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Hedge Fund Backed by Industry Leaders Closes Its Doors \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A hedge fund backed by some of the industry's biggest names, Cerrano Capital, is closing less than a year after it got off the ground, the latest sign of the difficulties new funds are having raising money. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Perrigo\f28 \uc2 \f2   to Separate Its Prescription Pharmaceutical Business \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Ireland-based \f28 \uc2 \f2 Perrigo\f28 \uc2 \f2  plans to shed its prescription pharmaceutical unit, which accounts for about one-fifth of total revenue, allowing the company to focus on expanding its consumer business. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Viacom\f28 \uc2 \f2  's Revenue Falls on Slump in Ad Sales \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Viacom\f28 \uc2 \f2  's revenue declined in the latest quarter as lower international sales from its filmed entertainment division, which includes \f28 \uc2 \f2 Paramount Pictures\f28 \uc2 \f2  , and advertising sales weighed on its top line. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Rite Aid\f28 \uc2 \f2  , \f28 \uc2 \f2 Albertsons\f28 \uc2 \f2   Call Off Merger \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Rite Aid and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Albertsons\f28 \uc2 \f2  have called off their planned merger in the face of mounting concern from investors. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Merck KGaA\f28 \uc2 \f2   Renews Commitment to U.S. Presence \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Merck KGaA\f28 \uc2 \f2  voiced concerns about barriers to free trade and highlighted a renewed commitment to its presence in the U.S., where it has been increasing its workforce. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Economists See Two More Fed Rate Increases This Year \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Most private economists see the Federal Reserve raising short-term interest rates two more times this year, once in September and again in December, according to The Wall Street Journal's monthly survey of forecasters. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Evans: Economy Supports Fed Moving to More Normal Rate Environment \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 9 Aug 2018 17:37 ET \par Asian Morning Briefing: U.S. Stocks End Mixed -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Chicago Fed\f28 \uc2 \f2  President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Charles Evans\f28 \uc2 \f2  said a strong economy, low unemployment and rising inflation mean the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  should be moving closer to a more normal interest-rate environment. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par RECENT DJ EXCLUSIVES \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Sumner Redstone\f28 \uc2 \f2  Trust Restricts Sale Options for \f28 \uc2 \f2 CBS\f28 \uc2 \f2  , \f28 \uc2 \f2 Viacom\f28 \uc2 \f2   \par Economists See Two More Fed Rate Increases This Year \par Growth Seen Hitting 3% in 2018, But Risks to Outlook Mount After This Year \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Merck KGaA\f28 \uc2 \f2  Renews Commitment to U.S. Presence Amid Trade Worries \par Hedge Fund Cerrano Capital Is Closing \par  \par TODAY'S CALENDAR \par (Times in GMT, followed by country and event) \par Friday, August 10, 2018 \par 0130  Australia    June       Lending Finance \par 0130  Australia               \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of Australia\f28 \uc2 \f2  Quarterly Statement on Monetary \par                               Policy 