\par\par \b \uc2 Global Equities Roundup: Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,315 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 August 2018\par 09:47\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 The latest Market Talks covering Equities. Published exclusively on Dow Jones Newswires throughout the day.\par \par \uc2 0747 GMT - Asian stock markets were all over the map Thursday, with local factors dominating amid a lack of overseas trading cues. Potential government \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in tech sent the sector higher in \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 , fueling gains of more than 2% for major indexes. That also helped Hong Kong. A stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  capped Japan's stocks while a weaker one helped fuel New Zealand's. Australia briefly hit another 10 1/2-year high on earnings news, but the Philippines fell 0.4%, well off session lows, after a weak GDP report and ahead of the imminent \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy statement. The moves came as India's Sensex continued to hit more record highs. S&P 500 futures are flat. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com; @kevinkingsbury)\par \par \uc2 0728 GMT - The roller-coaster ride that's been Chinese stocks of late continued today, and it was a climb. As the major indexes logged gains nearly as big as Tuesday's pops, the Shanghai Composite rose 1.8% to 2794.38 and the Shenzhen Composite jumped 2.7%. The startup-heavy ChiNext did top Tuesday's gain in bouncing 3.4%. Techs led, with \uc2 ZTE\uc2  logging a 10% limit-up gain along with more than 20 hardware, chip and 5G names amid potential of government action. The badly battered health-care sector also rebounded while energy stocks retreated on oil's overnight slump. With earnings season looming, \uc2 Citic Securities\uc2  says banks will likely be the upcoming focus amid slower deleveraging efforts. (john.wu@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Cheers,\par \par \uc2 John\par \par \uc2 0658 GMT - \uc2 Thyssenkrupp\uc2  's new long-term earnings and cash-flow targets aren't likely to persuade the market as long as short-term uncertainties remain, \uc2 Baader Bank\uc2  analyst Christian Obst says. The group published a target to hit EUR1 billion in free cash flow before mergers and acquisitions by its 2021 fiscal year alongside its third-quarter earnings. However, until the company finds a new CEO and supervisory board chairman, resolves the issues surrounding the proposed joint venture with \uc2 Tata Steel\uc2  and finds a way to bolster earnings at its industrial solutions unit, the targets will remain a secondary concern, he says. (nathan.allen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0649 GMT - Nordic stock markets are seen opening just in positive territory Thursday with IG calling the OMXS30 up 0.1% at around 1624. "With another quiet day on the economic data front, the market will focus on geopolitical developments around Russia and Turkey, where the U.S. has adopted new sanctions," says \uc2 Danske Bank\uc2  . The U.S. equity markets were range bound yesterday and it is again a mixed picture for Asian equity markets this morning. "China is preparing to retaliate against the rise in U.S. tariffs, and has announced that it will impose tariffs of 25% on $16 billion of U.S. goods imported by China." OMXS30 closed at 1622.63, OMXN40 at 1545.29 and OBX at 839.25. (dominic.chopping@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0648 GMT - \uc2 Zurich Insurance\uc2  's first-half net profit and operating profit came in above expectations, meaning there's a good chance of a healthy payout for shareholders, \uc2 Vontobel\uc2  says. "Zurich reports pleasing 1H 2018 results with solid progress on the non-life side and a good increase on the life-insurance side," it says. The Swiss insurance giant reported a 19% increase in net profit, which came in at $1.79 billion. Business operating profit also rose, standing at $2.42 billion compared with $2.17 billion a year earlier. "Zurich appears well on track to at least match last year's high dividend," \uc2 Vontobel\uc2  says. (pietro.lombardi@dowjones.com; @pietrolombard10)\par \par \uc2 0649 GMT - The global solar-energy market is maturing. Amid a curb on new approvals in China, the amount of solar-panel systems installed is set to fall 13% this year to 85 gigawatts of power production, predicts Tom Heggarty at \uc2 Wood Mackenzie\uc2 . A decline hasn't happened since 2000, he adds. Also capping the industry this year is a potential drop in India due to the recent imposition of import duties. China accounts for over half of the global solar market while India's market share is estimated to be around 9%. (biman.mukherji@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0648 GMT - Adidas's strong second-quarter results are above expectations and the company continues to outperform its U.S. peers, analysts at Baader Helvea say. Adidas's sales rose 10%, excluding \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  effects. Baader says Adidas's 16% growth in North America compares favorably with recent updates from \uc2 Nike\uc2  and \uc2 Under Armour\uc2  . "After 1H18, we see Adidas ahead of its full-year targets," Baader says. (anthony.shevlin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0647 GMT - \uc2 Thyssenkrupp\uc2  's target of EUR1 billion in free cash flow before mergers and acquisitions in 2021 is "the right number," according to \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  analyst Ingo Schachel. The group's mid-term margin targets for its individual units are roughly in line with expectations and with developments in their markets, he says. The challenge now is for the company to prove it can get there, particularly at its industrial-solutions unit, where the target implies a substantial improvement of the current cash conversion rate, the analyst says. (nathan.allen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0644 GMT - The FTSE 100 index is expected to open 8 points lower at 7,768, with concerns lingering about U.S.-China trade tensions after China announced plans for retaliatory trade tariffs. Any further weakness in the pound due to investor concerns about the risks that the U.K. could leave the \uc2 European Union\uc2  without an agreement in place, could lift stocks, however. The pound stabilizes after its sharp falls on Wednesday but remains below 1.29 against the dollar while the euro remains above 0.90 against the pound. Individual stocks in focus following earnings or trading updates include Randgold, \uc2 TUI\uc2  , \uc2 Zurich Insurance\uc2  , \uc2 Legal & General\uc2  , and \uc2 Coca-Cola HBC\uc2  . (jessica.fleetham@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0642 GMT - Though \uc2 Cathay Pacific\uc2  's 1H bottom line was much improved from a year earlier, management "sees pressure on passenger yields in 2H on intensifying competition," notes Daiwa. Then there's the higher fuel costs. But the investment bank remains bullish on valuation grounds, saying the stock "already reflects the concerns over rising fuel costs and partly factors in the impact of China-US trade tensions. Hence, we see limited downside risk going forward." However, that goes out the window if yields and traffic levels don't rise as expected. Falling 1.8% yesterday after the results, shares have rebounded 4.7% today to 3-week highs following the overnight swoon in oil prices. \uc2 Cathay\uc2  is up 2.5% for 2018. (john.wu@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0642 GMT - South Korea's stock benchmark struggled throughout Thursday's trading, unable to steadily stay above Wednesday's closing level as others in the region climbed. But thanks to a 3.5 point end-of-day adjustment higher, the Kospi finished up 0.07% at 2303.12. While chip maker \uc2 SK Hynix\uc2  fell a further 2.3% and steelmaker \uc2 Posco\uc2  shed 1.35%, \uc2 Korean Air\uc2  climbed 1.9% on the overnight skid in oil prices and \uc2 Korea Electric Power\uc2  bounced 2.8%. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com; @kevinkingsbury)\par \par \uc2 0632 GMT - \uc2 Adecco\uc2  's second-quarter results are broadly in line with expectations, analysts at \uc2 Vontobel\uc2  say. The Swiss company's 2Q revenue growth of 4%--adjusted for trading days--is marginally below expectations, with an acceleration in the U.S. offsetting slower growth in most European countries, \uc2 Vontobel\uc2  says. "Overall the trends are in line with expectations with overall growth similar to peers...we expect no significant changes to estimates and confirm our hold rating," \uc2 Vontobel\uc2  says. (anthony.shevlin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 09, 2018 03:47 ET (07:47 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020180809ee8900195\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
