\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Trading: China Props Up Yuan With Swaps -- WSJ\b0\par\par\uc2 By Shen Hong \par \uc2 926 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 August 2018\par 08:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par B13\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 This article is being republished as part of our daily reproduction of WSJ.com articles that also appeared in the U.S. print edition of The Wall Street Journal (August 8, 2018).\par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI -- The yuan's recent slide has been dramatic. Without some discreet expectations management on Beijing's part, it could have been even more striking.\par \par \uc2 Traders at four major financial institutions in Shanghai said the \uc2 People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  has shifted away from traditional \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , or selling billions of dollars to buy yuan, instead acting through lower-profile \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps.\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 PBOC\uc2  is guiding people's expectations in the forward market as it doesn't want to waste money in the spot market. Without action in the forward market, the yuan's depreciation could have been far worse," said Suan Teck Kin, an economist at United Overseas Bank.\par \par \uc2 Indeed, official data released on Tuesday showed that China's stockpile of foreign reserves actually rose slightly to about $3.12 trillion last month from $3.11 trillion in June -- a second straight month of increases. That stability helps bolster confidence in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , where a rapid decline could suggest large capital outflows, or determined \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action to prop it up.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has let the yuan fall more than 7% against the dollar since a trade brawl with Washington escalated in mid-June, which analysts said partly reflects a willingness to use yuan weakness to offset potentially higher U.S. tariffs on Chinese goods.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, traders said the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  aggressively in the market for so-called dollar-yuan \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps, using large state-owned banks as proxies. The action has helped imply a stronger Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and lower interest rates in the world's second-largest economy.\par \par \uc2 As in a typical \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -swap transaction, the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  buys dollars with yuan upfront and agrees to sell dollars a year from now. Such demand tends to drive down prices of forward transactions, which would imply a weaker future spot exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 Thanks to massive orders from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the benchmark one-year dollar-yuan swap spread has plunged. That means the current swap price implies a dollar-yuan exchange rate of 6.8064 in a year's time, compared with the spot rate of 6.8330 -- a negative difference of 0.0266 yuan. Two months ago, the difference was a positive 0.0770 yuan.\par \par \uc2 While the recent combination of falling Chinese and rising U.S. interest rates has also contributed to a narrowing swap spread, "it doesn't make sense for it to become negative," said \uc2 Zhou Hao\uc2 , an economist at \uc2 Commerzbank AG\uc2 . This is because Chinese interest rates remain higher than U.S. ones, implying a positive spread.\par \par \uc2 "Normally speaking, when the spot exchange rate is depreciating, the forward rate will depreciate even faster. But this time in China, the forward rate is implying yuan appreciation," said Mr. Zhou. "It feels like this market is under somebody's control."\par \par \uc2 This way, Beijing avoids a fast depletion of foreign reserves that would both aggravate the yuan's woes and exacerbate capital outflows. From 2015 to 2017, Beijing lost a quarter of its near-$4 trillion of foreign reserves as it fought to defend a weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "By buying dollars now and swapping them back in a year, the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  can use the effectively borrowed greenback to support the yuan without eroding foreign reserves," said the Shanghai-based head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at a mid-sized Chinese bank.\par \par \uc2 A swap trader at an Asian bank in Shanghai said: "It's a very cost-effective way to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and the stronger forward exchange rate is a warning signal for those who want to short the yuan aggressively."\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 PBOC\uc2  does face potential losses if the swap spread becomes less negative or re-enters positive territory in the coming days or months. However, such losses are considered far less significant than the cost it might incur from directly selling dollar reserves to salvage a plunging yuan.\par \par \uc2 This is not the \uc2 PBOC\uc2 's first use of this tactic.\par \par \uc2 It employed the same technique briefly in addition to direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the spot market in late August 2015, shortly after it shocked global markets with a surprise 2% one-off devaluation.\par \par \uc2 "It's a relatively new approach for the \uc2 PBOC\uc2 , but for other Asian central banks it's not unusual," said Mr. Suan at UOB, who noted that the Thai and Malaysian central banks often use \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 PBOC\uc2  took a step further on Friday by reimposing a requirement that makes it more expensive to bet that the yuan will weaken against the dollar in the forward market. Effective Monday, it said banks buying and selling dollar-denominated \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  forwards for clients will be asked to deposit 20% of their sales at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 However, the fact the yuan has largely maintained its downward momentum in the past two months shows that indirect \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  cannot be as effective as direct dollar sales.\par \par \uc2 "It gives people the impression that the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  didn't really intend to reverse the yuan's broad weakening trend. It just wants to slow the speed a bit and prevent its limited dollar sales from depleting the country's foreign reserves," said the Shanghai-based head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at a midsize Chinese bank.\par \par \uc2 Write to Shen Hong at hong.shen@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 08, 2018 02:32 ET (06:32 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020180808ee88000je\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
