\par\par \b \uc2 In Trade War Battle, China Shows It Can 'Take a Blow' -- Barron's Blog\b0\par\par\uc2 By Varada Bhat \par \uc2 377 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 August 2018\par 22:47\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 China's stock market rebounded on Tuesday after four straight days of losses amid fears of escalating trade tensions between Washington and Beijing. The release of China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, which unexpectedly rose in July, demonstrated that the world's second-largest economy can "take a blow."\par \par \uc2 The benchmark Shanghai Composite index jumped 2.7% to 2,705.16, while the blue-chip CSI 300 index rose 2.9%. The \uc2 iShares MSCI China Index Fund\uc2  (MCHI) climbed 1.7% to $62.29.\par \par \uc2 The rebound came after China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves rose by $5.8 billion in July to $3.118 trillion, compared with an increase of $1.51 billion in June, according to \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data.\par \par \uc2 Investors were closely monitoring this data for any indications of capital flowing out of the country, as trade tensions have hurt Chinese stocks and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The yuan has been one of the worst performing emerging markets \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  losing 6.4% in the past two months. At the same time, the U.S. dollar index rose 1.9%. "Amidst all the speculations of the yuan, China has shown that it has the power to retaliate and take a blow," said Jean Ergas, chief economist, Tigress Financial Partners.\par \par \uc2 Despite the market tumult in recent weeks, \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  appears comfortable with a weakening yuan and would selectively \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  only to prevent any destabilizing declines or to restore market confidence, Ergas told Barron's.\par \par \uc2 Last week, the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  announced that traders would require traders to place reserves with an equivalent to 20% of their positions to trade some \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  forward contracts, making it expensive to short the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Beijing introduced the 20% requirement in 2015 to help stabilize the yuan after Beijing unexpectedly devalued it.\par \par \uc2 Sign up to Review & Preview, a new daily email from Barron's. Every evening we'll review the news that moved markets during the day and look ahead to what it means for your portfolio in the morning. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  More at Barron's Focus on Funds blog, \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.barrons.com/focus-on-funds"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.barrons.com/focus-on-funds}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 August 07, 2018 16:47 ET (20:47 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020180807ee87003rs\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
