\par\par \b \uc2 Indian Morning Briefing: Asian Markets Mostly Higher\b0\par\par\uc2 1,071 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 August 2018\par 05:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GLOBAL MARKETS \par DJIA         25462.58  136.42  0.54% \par Nasdaq         7812.01    9.33  0.12% \par S&P 500       2840.35   13.13  0.46% \par FTSE 100      7659.10   83.17  1.10% \par Nikkei Stock 22606.83   81.65  0.36% \par Hang Seng    28028.01  351.69  1.27% \par Kospi         2298.98   11.30  0.49% \par SGX Nifty    11437.50   40.00  0.35% \par August contract \par  \par USD/JPY  111.31-32   +0.05% \par Range    111.36   111.17 \par EUR/USD  1.1563-66   -0.03% \par Range    1.1570   1.1558 \par  \par CBOT  Wheat Sept $5.562 per bushel \par Comex Gold     $1,214.20  +0.3% \par Nymex  Crude (NY) $68.64  -$0.32 \par  \par  \par U.S. STOCKS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stocks rose, lifting the S&P 500 to its fifth consecutive weekly gain, after a string of upbeat earnings helped reassure investors that the market is on solid footing.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average added 136.42 points, or 0.5%, to 25462.58. The S&P 500 was up 13.13 points, or 0.5%, to 2840.35 while the Nasdaq Composite edged up 9.33 points, or 0.1%, to 7812.01.\par \par \uc2 For the week, the Dow industrials rose less than 0.1%, the S&P 500 rose 0.8% and the Nasdaq advanced 1%.\par \par \uc2 Stocks were steady after Labor Department data Friday showed U.S. nonfarm payrolls rose a seasonally adjusted 157,000 in July, less than the 190,000 that economists surveyed by The Wall Street Journal had expected. Still, over a broader period, job growth looks strong, analysts said-with the pace of hiring through the first seven months of this year rising from last year's average through July.\par \par \uc2 Robust economic data and corporate earnings have helped keep investors relatively optimistic in recent months, even as global trade tensions have ratcheted higher. After reporting solid revenue and profit gains this week, \uc2 Apple\uc2  became the first U.S. company to surpass $1 trillion in market value.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, \uc2 Kellogg\uc2  shares jumped $2.27, or 3.2%, to $72.15 after the cereal maker raised its full-year guidance Thursday, and \uc2 Dish Network\uc2  soared 4.34, or 15%, to 34.20 after posting better-than-expected results.\par \par \uc2 "What we can trade on is earnings, and it's been a good earnings season," said JJ Kinahan, chief market strategist at \uc2 TD Ameritrade\uc2 . "Until we have proof that something else is happening, I think you have to continue on with that thought." \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par ASIAN STOCKS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Strength seen in Europe and the Americas on Friday has carried over into Asia to start the week, with some beaten-down markets leading the gains. Indexes in Hong Kong and Singapore are up more than 1% after sizable declines in both markets last week as they underperformed most. Trade worries hit both those trade-dependent locales, and that was the case again this morning in China with Shenzhen benchmarks briefly falling more than 1%. They're now down less than 0.5%, lone decliners in the region, while the country's large caps are modestly higher. Gains of more than 0.5% are being logged in Australia, New Zealand and Indonesia. S&P 500 futures have also improved a bit, with the session's gain now at 0.15%. \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 More \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may come from Beijing to support a sliding yuan, said \f28 \uc2 \f2 OCBC\f28 \uc2 \f2 's \f28 \uc2 \f2 Tommy Xie\f28 \uc2 \f2  following late Friday's \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  announcement to restart a 20% reserve for long dollar-forward positions. "The reserve was not a gamechanger back in 2015 as it failed to stop RMB from weakening further. But it is a strong signal." He adds the move suggests that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  won't hesitate to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market should \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  depreciation risk financial stability. A next step could include the restart of counter-cyclical factors, said Xie.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China guided the yuan 0.3% weaker against the dollar, keeping the Chinese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at its lowest since May 31, 2017. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  fixed the dollar's midpoint for daily trading at CNY6.8513, compared with Friday's CNY6.8322. The yuan ended the week's onshore trading at CNY6.8620, though it later rebounded offshore in late Hong Kong trading Friday after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said it would reimpose a requirement that makes it more expensive to bet on declines in the yuan. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par METALS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 London spot-gold prices have started the week in Asia with gains, extending Friday's uptick amid the U.S. jobs report. The yellow metal, which remains near 1-year lows, is also starting to attract some physical buying. "Gold has started to find a bid," said Ryan Case, head of sales at Allocated Bullion Exchange. He notes there's been an increase in demand for small gold bars in Asia. Spot gold was recently up 0.2% at $1,217.05/troy ounce. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par OIL SUMMARY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Oil futures are generally quiet to start the week in Asia after Friday's drop capped a 4th down week in 5 for the crude market. Both the WTI and Brent benchmarks are down some 8% from their latest 3 1/2-year highs, hurt to varying degrees by factors including a strong dollar and still-robust supplies. But so long as demand remains stout itself, downside is liable to be limited. September WTI was recently up 12 cents at $68.61/barrel and October Brent was off 2 cents at $73.19. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TOP HEADLINES \par  \par Asian Nations Push Back at U.S. on Trade, Sanctions \par Senators Signal Concerns Over China's Global Investments \par Scores Killed in Latest Indonesian Earthquake \par Saudi Arabia Expels Canadian Ambassador \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Goldman Sachs\f28 \uc2 \f2  to Name New Trading Co-Head Amid Reboot \par Profits Surge at Big U.S. Firms \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Samsung\f28 \uc2 \f2  Tries to Navigate Through U.S.-China Trade Crossfire \par Warren Buffett's Berkshire Hathaway  Profit Surges \par U.S. Officials Push New Penalties for Hackers of Electrical Grid \par Trump Defends Son's Meeting With Russian Lawyer \par South Sudan Leaders Make Another Attempt at Peace \par Iranians Hoard Gold Ahead of U.S. Sanctions \par Turkey Responds to U.S. Sanctions Over Pastor's Detainment \par North Korea Warns U.S. of Difficulties Over Nuclear Deal \par U.S. to Press Russia Over Potential Violations of North Korea Sanctions \par Trump Replies to 'Nice' Letter From North Korea's Kim \par Canada's Freeland Vows to Work Toward Quick Nafta Resolution \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 August 05, 2018 23:00 ET (03:00 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020180806ee86000f1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
