\par\par \b \uc2 China May Raise Rate to Ease Outflow Pressure -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,006 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 August 2018\par 05:40\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0340 GMT - Despite China's slowing economic-growth momentum, ANZ contends a small hike in the 7-day reverse-repo rate remains a logical policy option due to exacerbating outflow pressure. "Given market expectations of further easing measures in China and more US Fed rate hikes, the chances of a negative China-US bond yield spread are mounting," the investment bank notes. Ten-year Treasurys currently yield just below 3% while Chinese sovereign debt is around 3.5% after sliding from 4% early this year. Meanwhile, ANZ says the \uc2 PBoC\uc2 's Friday night announcement to restart a 20% reserve for long dollar-forward positions reflects authorities' concerns about outflows via the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  forward market amid the yuan's weakness. (liyan.qi@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0253 GMT - There is a growing body of opinion that the softening New Zealand economy will necessitate the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  moving to a more-dovish stance. But \uc2 BNZ\uc2  rejects that idea as inflation and inflationary pressures are on the rise while the local economy is at a point of maximum sustainable employment. The investment bank contends that if the \uc2 RBNZ\uc2  could be convinced that recent data like falling business confidence will lead to falling inflation, only then could a case for lower interest rates be built. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0210 GMT - More \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may come from Beijing to support a sliding yuan, says \uc2 OCBC\uc2 's \uc2 Tommy Xie\uc2  following late Friday's PBoC announcement to restart a 20% reserve for long dollar-forward positions. "The reserve was not a gamechanger back in 2015 as it failed to stop RMB from weakening further. But it is a strong signal." He adds the move suggests that \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't hesitate to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market should \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciation risk financial stability. A next step could include the restart of counter-cyclical factors, says Xie. (liyan.qi@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0132 GMT - Australian job advertisements bounced 1.5% seasonally-adjusted in July, nearly reversing the 1.7% fall recorded last month, says ANZ. On an annual basis, growth ticked up to 7.3% in July from 6.9% in June. In trend terms, average monthly growth slowed from 0.8% in 1Q to 0.2% in 2Q. (james.glynn@wsj.com ; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0024 GMT - Among the different narratives which have emerged after last week's BoJ meeting, \uc2 Merrill Lynch\uc2  is leaning toward the "dovish camp." That's because while near-term volatility with the JGB market is capping dollar-yen until yields settle down, the investment bank expects "the flow dynamic to remain supportive" for the cross while policy and data divergence will likely drive the pair higher. Meanwhile, \uc2 Merrill\uc2  says last week's policy tweaks are "unlikely to be a game changer" for dollar-yen. The greenback is at Y111.15, versus Y111.25 when Tokyo's stock market opened and above Y111.50 during Asian trading on Friday. The yen's uptick has helped turn Japanese stocks lower after opening modestly higher. (suryatapa.bhattacharya@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0123 GMT - China guides the yuan 0.3% weaker against the dollar, keeping the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at its lowest since May 31, 2017. The \uc2 PBoC\uc2  fixed the dollar's midpoint for daily trading at CNY6.8513, compared with Friday's CNY6.8322. The yuan ended the week's onshore trading at CNY6.8620, though it later rebounded offshore in late Hong Kong trading Friday after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it would reimpose a requirement that makes it more expensive to bet on declines in the yuan. (saumya.vaishampayan@wsj.com; @saumvaish)\par \par \uc2 2359 GMT - Thursday's policy statement from \uc2 New Zealand's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should highlight the current environment's complicated nature with economic growth slowing and inflation rising, says ANZ. Policy deliberations this week are liable to be focused on how officials see the balance of risks. ANZ says for now, focusing too much on CPI or simply tallying up economic developments run the potential of missing important nuances. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 2358 GMT - Japanese stocks are set to open modestly higher with Nikkei futures up 0.3%. That despite the dollar easing to Y111.20 from near Y111.70 when Friday's local stock trading ended. Meanwhile, traders continue to keep an eye on bond yields. They eased for Treasurys after the Friday US jobs report and are around 2.96% this morning in Asia for the 10-year. The 10-year JGB is down a half-basis point at 0.1% after getting as high as 0.13% on Friday. (suryatapa.bhattacharya@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 [Dow Jones ] Australian business confidence was down 0.8 points (-0.7%) to 113.6 in July, according to \uc2 Roy Morgan Research\uc2  . Confidence has dropped below its level of a year ago for the first time in 2018 and is now 3.4 points lower than in July 2017 and 2.7 points below its long-term average of 116.3. Still, businesses remain largely positive with a majority, 50.9%, saying now is a good time to invest and 50.5% expecting good times for the Australian economy over the next 5 years. (James.Glynn@WSJ.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 [Dow Jones ] The \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2  is expected to keep its forecasts for inflation and growth mostly unchanged this week. Official 2Q CPI data showed inflation tracking to where the \uc2 RBA\uc2  had forecast in May. The \uc2 RBA\uc2  is set to remain upbeat on the growth outlook, pointing to robust business conditions and investment as evidence. Still, the outlook for interest rates is subdued. With wages growth still flat, the \uc2 RBA\uc2  will continue to emphasize a gradual rise in inflation is likely. Markets are pricing a 2020 interest rate hike. This week's \uc2 RBA\uc2  policy meeting on Tuesday and forecasts on Friday won't upset that assumption. (James.Glynn@WSJ.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 05, 2018 23:40 ET (03:40 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020180806ee86000fi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
