\par\par \b \uc2 SE Asia Equities May be a Safe Bet -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,307 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 August 2018\par 08:37\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0637 GMT - Amid worries about trade and China's economy and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \uc2 Nomura\uc2  says Southeast Asian equities offer some relative safety. While the region's economies are trade-reliant, their stock benchmarks have a preponderance of sectors which lean domestic-focused. Meanwhile, many of the markets are lightly owned by international investors while earnings expectations are modest and valuations appear attractive. \uc2 Nomura\uc2  goes on to say that while weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the region have been an issue with the dollar's broad strength, they may find support from Southeast Asian central banks turning more hawkish amid inflation concerns. (saurabh.chaturvedi@wsj.com; @journosaurabh)\par \par \uc2 0622 GMT - With the yen broadly stronger than end-of-week levels, Japanese stocks paused while many in Asia have logged gains today. The Nikkei eased 0.1% to 22507.32 after opening modestly higher to start the week and struggling much of the day. That as 10-year JGB yields eased to 0.1% from 0.105% earlier. \uc2 MUFG\uc2  dropped 2.3% as bank shares on the Topix were among the day's biggest losers, pulling back after strength seen much of last week. But SoftBank rose 2.2% ahead of its quarterly report. Tomorrow, investors will be keeping an eye on key economic data including June household spending and wages--key indicators for how the economy is faring. (suryatapa.bhattacharya@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0547 GMT - As \uc2 Nomura\uc2  expects China to likely ratchet up policy-easing efforts as the investment bank anticipates economic growth there to "deteriorate at a faster pace in the next couple of months...the pace and intensity" is liable to be data-dependent. Obviously the trade fight with the US is another key factor, but for now "it is difficult to gauge when growth will bottom out and when Beijing will become more decisive in its easing/stimulus." (john.wu@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0541 GMT - \uc2 State Bank of India\uc2  has raised $200 million through a private bond deal, a detour from its earlier plans of raising $500 million through what would have been its initial green-bond sale. It's a sign that issuers remain sensitive to higher funding costs. The private deal was wrapped up last month via a foreign bank, with $50 million of 5-year bonds paying a coupon of 4.35% and $150 million of 18-month floating-rate notes paying 0.85 percentage point above 3-month Libor, according to sources familiar with the situation. India's biggest lender met fixed-income investors in early July via 7 banks. It isn't clear if the new bonds are green ones. (manju.dalal@wsj.com; @manjudalalsg)\par \par \uc2 0415 GMT - Will the back-and-forth between the U.S. and China on trade be decided by the Nov. 6 midterm election? There's probably little reason economically during the next few months for either side to back down from their plans to slap more tariffs on each other's imports, even though some of last week's China \uc2 PMI\uc2  data for July hinted at trade impacts. Significant effects are liable to take longer to develop, though. But politics is likely to be the bigger reason behind the lack of a trade deal by November. While the GOP losing both houses of Congress would weaken the White House's negotiating positioning, Trump would be emboldened to keep the pressure on Beijing if Republicans hold on. He would likely extol that trade is a winning issue for the party. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com; @kevinkingsbury)\par \par \uc2 0351 GMT - [Dow Jones ] \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  expects the \uc2 Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise its benchmark rate by 50 basis points to 4.0% at its upcoming meeting on Thursday given recent hawkish comments and upside surprises to inflation. "In our view, the economy is overheating from expansionary fiscal and monetary policy," with tightening required to restore macro-stability and slow domestic demand to keep inflation and the current account deficit in check, says the broker. It also notes rapid credit growth in the past five years, higher than in other ASEAN 4 economies. (kenan.machado@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0340 GMT - Despite China's slowing economic-growth momentum, ANZ contends a small hike in the 7-day reverse-repo rate remains a logical policy option due to exacerbating outflow pressure. "Given market expectations of further easing measures in China and more US Fed rate hikes, the chances of a negative China-US bond yield spread are mounting," the investment bank notes. Ten-year Treasurys currently yield just below 3% while Chinese sovereign debt is around 3.5% after sliding from 4% early this year. Meanwhile, ANZ says the \uc2 PBoC\uc2 's Friday night announcement to restart a 20% reserve for long dollar-forward positions reflects authorities' concerns about outflows via the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  forward market amid the yuan's weakness. (liyan.qi@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0253 GMT - There is a growing body of opinion that the softening New Zealand economy will necessitate the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  moving to a more-dovish stance. But \uc2 BNZ\uc2  rejects that idea as inflation and inflationary pressures are on the rise while the local economy is at a point of maximum sustainable employment. The investment bank contends that if the \uc2 RBNZ\uc2  could be convinced that recent data like falling business confidence will lead to falling inflation, only then could a case for lower interest rates be built. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0210 GMT - More \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may come from Beijing to support a sliding yuan, says \uc2 OCBC\uc2 's \uc2 Tommy Xie\uc2  following late Friday's PBoC announcement to restart a 20% reserve for long dollar-forward positions. "The reserve was not a gamechanger back in 2015 as it failed to stop RMB from weakening further. But it is a strong signal." He adds the move suggests that \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't hesitate to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market should \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciation risk financial stability. A next step could include the restart of counter-cyclical factors, says Xie. (liyan.qi@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0132 GMT - Australian job advertisements bounced 1.5% seasonally-adjusted in July, nearly reversing the 1.7% fall recorded last month, says ANZ. On an annual basis, growth ticked up to 7.3% in July from 6.9% in June. In trend terms, average monthly growth slowed from 0.8% in 1Q to 0.2% in 2Q. (james.glynn@wsj.com ; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0024 GMT - Among the different narratives which have emerged after last week's BoJ meeting, \uc2 Merrill Lynch\uc2  is leaning toward the "dovish camp." That's because while near-term volatility with the JGB market is capping dollar-yen until yields settle down, the investment bank expects "the flow dynamic to remain supportive" for the cross while policy and data divergence will likely drive the pair higher. Meanwhile, \uc2 Merrill\uc2  says last week's policy tweaks are "unlikely to be a game changer" for dollar-yen. The greenback is at Y111.15, versus Y111.25 when Tokyo's stock market opened and above Y111.50 during Asian trading on Friday. The yen's uptick has helped turn Japanese stocks lower after opening modestly higher. (suryatapa.bhattacharya@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0123 GMT - China guides the yuan 0.3% weaker against the dollar, keeping the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at its lowest since May 31, 2017. The \uc2 PBoC\uc2  fixed the dollar's midpoint for daily trading at CNY6.8513, compared with Friday's CNY6.8322. The yuan ended the week's onshore trading at CNY6.8620, though it later rebounded offshore in late Hong Kong trading Friday after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it would reimpose a requirement that makes it more expensive to bet on declines in the yuan. (saumya.vaishampayan@wsj.com; @saumvaish)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 06, 2018 02:37 ET (06:37 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020180806ee86000oa\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
