US$         14,455         0           INR US$             68.655       -0.07\par  MYR US$         4.058          0.005       TWD US$             30.573       -0.059\par  CNY US$         6.7845         0.0207      HKD US$             7.8482       0.0029\par  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\par --     \par  Commodities                                                                 \par  Spot Gold       1,224.46       -6.7        Silver (Lon)        15.422       -0.135\par  U.S. Gold Fut   1,223.9        -7.9        Brent Crude         74.66        0.73\par  Iron Ore        CNY488         13          TRJCRB Index        -            -\par  TOCOM Rubber    JPY167.4       0.3         \f28 \uc2 \f2 LME\f28 \uc2 \f2  Copper          6,281.5      -8.5\par  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\par --  \par     ** indicates closing price \par     All prices as of 18:18 GMT\par     \par     EQUITIES\par \par     GLOBAL - World stock markets struggled to hold on to four-month highs on Thursday\par as a record sell-off in shares of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Facebook Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2  offset optimism that the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par and the United States would settle their differences on trade.\par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 Facebook Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2 , the fifth-largest global stock by market capitalization,\par collapsed 18.89 percent, set for the biggest one-day wipeout in U.S. stock market\par history, after the social media company's earnings report showed slowing usage in the\par biggest advertising markets. \par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     NEW YORK - \f28 \uc2 \f2 Facebook\f28 \uc2 \f2 's slump set the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nasdaq\f28 \uc2 \f2  on pace for its biggest drop in a month,\par but the contagion was contained by a rise in trade-sensitive stocks after the United\par States and the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2  said they would negotiate on trade.\par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 Facebook\f28 \uc2 \f2  shares dived 18.5 percent, set for its biggest one-day percentage\par drop ever, after the social media giant said profit margins would plummet for years due\par to costs to improve privacy safeguards and slowing usage in its big advertising\par markets.\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     LONDON - European shares rose to a six-week high on Thursday as hopes of a\par breakthrough in U.S.-EU trade talks boosted carmakers, while the market also digested a\par flurry of company earnings reports.\par     The pan-European STOXX 600 index ended up 0.9 percent, further supported\par late in the session by a fall in the euro after ECB President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mario Draghi\f28 \uc2 \f2  reaffirmed a\par commitment to keep interest rates low through next summer.\par     For a full report, click on\par \par     - - - -\par     \par     TOKYO - Most Japanese shares closed higher on Thursday on upbeat earnings and\par easing concerns about trade tensions, though the Nikkei benchmark softened on\par speculation the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  might trim buying of exchange traded funds linked to the\par index.\par     The broader Topix ended the day at five-week highs at 1,765.78, rallying\par 0.7 percent, with more than 1,700 out of 2,100 listed shares posting rises.\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     SHANGHAI - China stocks ended lower on Thursday as months of see-sawing U.S.-Sino\par trade friction stoked uncertainties over the country's economic growth, prompting\par investors to take a cautious stance.\par     The blue-chip CSI300 index ended down 1.2 percent at 3,536.25 points,\par while the Shanghai Composite Index closed down 0.7 percent at 2,882.23 points.\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     AUSTRALIA - Australian shares were little changed at the close on Thursday as\par merger activity in the media industry and news of the appointment of a new chief\par executive at Macquarie Group did little to support the broader market.\par     Financials slipped after \f28 \uc2 \f2 Macquarie Group\f28 \uc2 \f2  promoted veteran company\par executive Shemara Wikramanayake to be its first female chief executive, as the bank\par coupled its announcement of personnel change at the top with a forecast of flat profit\par growth in 2019. The stock closed down 2.6 percent.\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     SEOUL - South Korea's KOSPI stock index and the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  won rose on\par Thursday after U.S. President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Donald Trump\f28 \uc2 \f2  and European Commission President\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Jean-Claude Juncker\f28 \uc2 \f2  agreed to work towards eliminating trade barriers, easing immediate\par concerns about worsening global trade tensions. South Korean bond yields fell.\par     At 06:33 GMT, the KOSPI was up 16.03 points, or 0.71 percent, at 2,289.06. Gains\par were led by chipmaker \f28 \uc2 \f2 SK Hynix\f28 \uc2 \f2 , which closed 3.2 percent higher after the\par company said second-quarter operating profit surged 83 percent from a year earlier and\par beat market estimates.\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREIGN EXCHANGE\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par \par     NEW YORK - The euro fell on Thursday as the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  clung to its easy\par monetary policy and signaled no change in its timetable to move away from its ultra low\par rate policy and to end its asset bond purchase program.\par     An index that tracks the dollar versus six major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was up 0.3\par percent at 94.640, rebounding from a two-week low reached earlier Thursday.\par     For a full report, click on\par \par     - - - -\par     \par     SHANGHAI - Large state-run Chinese banks sold dollars in the yuan forwards market\par on Thursday, with their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  causing the future value of the yuan against the\par dollar to rise sharply, traders said.\par     There was little evidence of these banks, which are widely believed to often act on\par behalf of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 People's Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , having \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the spot market, traders\par said, even though spot yuan extended its slow gains during the day, rising as far as\par 6.7778 per dollar compared to this week's 13-month low of 6.8295.\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     AUSTRALIA - The Australian and New Zealand dollars rallied on Thursday as an easing\par in trade tensions between the United States and \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2  sparked a revival in\par risk appetite, at least until the next negative news headline came along.\par     The Aussie dollar had clambered up to $0.7443, from a low of $0.7392 on\par Wednesday, but met stiff resistance around $0.7465. It also faces a daunting chart\par barrier at $0.7484 which marks a double top from earlier in the month.\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     SEOUL - The South Korean won on Thursday was quoted at 1,119.3 per dollar on the\par onshore settlement platform, 0.63 percent firmer than its previous close at\par 1,126.3, extending gains into a second session.\par     In offshore trading, the South Korean won was quoted at 1,119.26 per U.S.\par dollar, down 0.23 percent from the previous day, while in one-year non-deliverable\par forwards it was being asked at 1,103.85 per dollar.\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     TREASURIES\par     \par     NEW YORK - U.S. benchmark Treasury yields fell from six-week highs on Thursday as\par they hit technical resistance, but remained elevated after an agreement between the\par United States and \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2  on Wednesday eased some trade tensions.\par     U.S. President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Donald Trump\f28 \uc2 \f2  agreed on Wednesday to refrain from imposing car\par tariffs on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2  while the two sides negotiate to cut other trade\par barriers, easing the threat of a transatlantic trade war.\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     LONDON - The euro fell, stocks extended gains and bond yields pulled off their\par highs on Thursday, after \f28 \uc2 \f2 ECB\f28 \uc2 \f2  boss \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mario Draghi\f28 \uc2 \f2  reaffirmed a commitment to keep interest\par rates low "through" next summer, even though he saw inflation picking up by the end of\par the year.\par     After the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , as expected, kept interest rates on hold, Draghi\par told reporters inflation uncertainty was receding, though he cautioned it was "too\par early to call victory".\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     TOKYO - The benchmark 10-year Japanese government bond yield rose to the highest in\par a year on Thursday as concerns that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  might be moving closer to\par unwinding its massive monetary stimulus continued dogging the debt market.\par     The 10-year yield was 3 basis points higher at 0.095 percent after\par brushing 0.100 percent, its highest since July 2017.\par     For a full report, click on\par  \par     \par     COMMODITIES\par     \par     GOLD\par \par     Gold edged lower on Thursday under pressure from a stronger dollar and in response\par to easing trade tensions after the United States agreed to refrain from imposing\par tariffs on cars from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\par     The two sides will also begin talks to cut other trade barriers, they announced on\par Wednesday following a meeting at the White House.\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     IRON ORE\par     \par     China's construction steel rebar prices recovered after some losses in early\par trading on Thursday, extending a three-day winning streak although investors' worries\par about demand returned, which may offset expectations that the environmental crackdown\par in the world's top steelmaker will tighten supplies.\par     The most-active rebar on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Shanghai Futures Exchange\f28 \uc2 \f2  closed up 0.3\par percent at 4,017 yuan ($592.30) a tonne. On Tuesday, the market hit a high of 4,033\par yuan a tonne, its strongest since early September.\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     BASE METALS\par     \par     Base metals steadied on Thursday after the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2  and the United States\par agreed to negotiate on trade, easing fears of a trade war, though worries over growth\par in top consumer China kept gains in check.\par     In what European Commission President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Jean-Claude Juncker\f28 \uc2 \f2  called a "major\par concession", U.S. President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Donald Trump\f28 \uc2 \f2  agreed on Wednesday to refrain from imposing\par car tariffs while the two sides launch negotiations to cut other trade barriers. \par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     OIL\par     \par     Oil prices rose for the third consecutive day on Thursday after Saudi Arabia\par suspended oil shipments through a strait in the Red Sea and as trade tensions between\par the United States and the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2  eased.\par     Brent futures rose 57 cents to $74.50 a barrel by 11:42 a.m. EDT (1542\par GMT). The contract earlier touched $74.83 a barrel, highest since July 16. U.S. West\par Texas Intermediate (WTI) crude futures were up 41 cents at $69.71.\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     PALM OIL\par     \par     Malaysian palm oil futures climbed roughly 2 percent on Thursday, supported by\par gains in rival U.S. soyoil and as a price correction to a near three-year low boosted\par export demand.\par     The benchmark palm oil contract for October delivery on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bursa\par Malaysia\f28 \uc2 \f2  Derivatives Exchange was up 1.76 percent at 2,201 ringgit ($542.39) a tonne at\par the end of the trading day, after rising 0.5 percent on Wednesday.\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     RUBBER\par     \par     Benchmark Tokyo rubber futures dropped on Thursday amid worries over demand in\par China, the world's biggest buyer, due to mounting trade tensions, while a blast outside\par the U.S. embassy in Beijing raised concerns over political stability.\par     A bomb exploded outside the U.S. embassy on Thursday, wounding the lone assailant,\par the embassy said in a statement, but police described the weapon merely as a "firework\par device".\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par \par  (Bengaluru Bureau; +91 80 6749 1130)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2018-7-26T19:22:20.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020180726ee7q00p5o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
