\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Major state-owned Chinese banks sell dollars in yuan forwards market\b0\par\par\uc2 638 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Juli 2018\par 12:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2018 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * State-run banks swap dollars for yuan in one-year forwards\par \par \uc2 * Traders unsure if they sold equivalent amount of spot dollars\par \par \uc2 * Analysts confused about \uc2 PBOC\uc2  intent in making forward dollars cheap\par \par \uc2 * They say easy policy bias justifies move in yuan forwards (Adds background, quotes)\par \par \uc2 By Winni Zhou and John Ruwitch\par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI, July 26 (Reuters) - Large state-run Chinese banks sold dollars in the yuan forwards market on Thursday, with their \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  causing the future value of the yuan against the dollar to rise sharply, traders said.\par \par \uc2 There was little evidence of these banks, which are widely believed to often act on behalf of the \uc2 People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 , having \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the spot market, traders said, even though spot yuan extended its slow gains during the day, rising as far as 6.7778 per dollar compared to this week's 13-month low of 6.8295.\par \par \uc2 The yuan's steady 7-percent decline since April has been driven by worries over China's simmering trade spat with the United States as well as rising U.S. market yields and a strengthening dollar.\par \par \uc2 While state banks have sporadically stepped into the market during the yuan's decline from a peak of around 6.24 in late March, at times combining spot dollar sales with swaps to replenish those dollars, traders and analysts couldn't explain the rationale for Thursday's heavy activity in dollar-yuan swaps.\par \par \uc2 Ken Cheung, senior Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Mizuho Bank\uc2  in Hong Kong, said he suspected the action in the forwards market was linked to China's current easy monetary policy bias. That meant it could not, unlike during the yuan's steep decline in 2015 and 2016, keep selling dollars for yuan and thus depleting yuan balances in the banking system.\par \par \uc2 "But now China remains in a process of deleveraging, and inadequate liquidity could cause some defaults. I think China wants to have an independent monetary policy," Cheung said.\par \par \uc2 Multiple traders said state-run banks were seen swapping large amounts of dollars for yuan in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  forwards.\par \par \uc2 Forward dollar selling by the big banks drove down one-year tenor of the dollar/yuan swap to as low as 0 points on Thursday afternoon, compared with the previous close of 110 points. That effectively meant one-year yuan was priced at the same rate as spot yuan.\par \par \uc2 Traders said the sharp fall in swap points was justified by the shrinking interest rate spreads between U.S. and Chinese rates as the Fed sustains its policy tightening and China eases.\par \par \uc2 While lower swap costs could make it easier to short-sell the yuan, traders also said the authorities seemed to be tempering depreciation expectations by containing the price of yuan in the forwards market.\par \par \uc2 "It seems that China wants to weaken its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to promote exports, while authorities are also afraid of reigniting capital outflows," Cheung said.\par \par \uc2 The sharp moves in the forward market were also on account of poor liquidity as dollar buyers stayed on the sidelines, another trader said.\par \par \uc2 "You never know where \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's bottom line is, so no one dares to buy. Everyone is watching," said a trader at a foreign bank in Shanghai.\par \par \uc2 The strong move in one-year dollar/yuan swaps gave rise to expectations that the swap curve could turn negative, meaning one-year dollars would be cheaper than spot dollars.\par \par \uc2 "Big banks were selling in the long end. And such offering has dragged the swap points lower," said a trader at an Asian bank in Shanghai.\par \par \uc2 "It could fall further to negative, depending on when the big banks stop selling." (Reporting by Winni Zhou and John Ruwitch Writing by Vidya Ranganathan; Editing by Richard Borsuk) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2018-7-26T11:59:38.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020180726ee7q00dbi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
