\par\par \b \uc2 Why Treasury Hasn't Labeled China, EU  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Manipulators\b0\par\par\uc2 By Josh Zumbrun \par \uc2 884 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Juli 2018\par 07:51\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  said Friday that "China, the \uc2 European Union\uc2  and others have been manipulating their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and interest rates lower, " returning the spotlight to the issue.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump was alleging that these economies take policy measures to lower the value of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  relative to others to make their exports cheaper on world markets. The U.S., by contrast, allows the value of the dollar to be determined by global financial markets.\par \par \uc2 As a candidate, Mr. Trump repeatedly promised to designate China a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator -- a label that would potentially open the country up to economic penalties. But his Treasury Department has three times declined to do so. The action would come in one of the Treasury's semiannual \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reports; the next one is scheduled for October.\par \par \uc2 Treasury hasn't labeled any nation a manipulator because none of them meets its three-part test. These are the three components:\par \par \uc2 1.) An economy must have a significant trade surplus with the U.S. of at least $20 billion. China and the \uc2 European Union\uc2  easily clear this hurdle. China's is over $300 billion and the \uc2 EU\uc2 's is over $100 billion (with Germany responsible for about $66 billion).\par \par \uc2 2.) The economy must have an overall current account surplus of at least 3% of its gross domestic product. In other words, the country must have a large trade surplus not just with the U.S., but with the entire world, too. The \uc2 EU\uc2  and Germany easily meet this threshold. In April, the \uc2 EU\uc2  had a current account surplus of 3.5% of GDP. For Germany, the figure was 8.1%.\par \par \uc2 But China's current account surplus has been shrinking. Despite running a massive surplus with the U.S., its total global surplus was just 1.4% in the twelve months through April, according to Treasury's measure. China increasingly imports from the rest of the world, and it has a large deficit when it comes to tourism -- a report from Nielsen estimates Chinese tourists took 131 million trips to other countries, mostly elsewhere in Asia. So China doesn't meet this threshold.\par \par \uc2 3.) To be a manipulator, an economy must conduct "persistent, one-sided \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 " in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. On this score, neither the \uc2 EU\uc2  nor China comes close.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which sets monetary policy for most of the countries in the \uc2 EU\uc2 , does not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets at all. Low interest rates have contributed to a weaker euro, of course, but monetary policies primarily aimed at boosting economic growth have not traditionally been considered manipulation.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets but over the past year it has not done so on a large scale and not in a single direction. Under the Treasury's framework, if China buys a certain amount of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and sells the same amount, the two moves would cancel each other out. Over recent months, China's moves have had little net impact. Though China has weakened its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the past month -- some suspect as a strategy to offset tariffs -- earlier in the year it had taken steps to let its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthen.\par \par \uc2 "The data doesn't make a strong case for a determination of manipulation" by China, said \uc2 Brad Setser\uc2 , senior fellow for international economics at \uc2 the Council on Foreign Relations\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Simply put, neither China nor the \uc2 EU\uc2  meet all three of Treasury criteria.\par \par \uc2 Treasury could simply move its goal posts. \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation is covered by two U.S. laws in particular. The department's report sticks to a framework from the 2015 Trade Facilitation and Trade Enforcement Act. But there's an older law, the 1988 Omnibus Trade and Competitiveness Act, which has much more flexible definitions. By switching to definitions under the old law, the Trump administration could easily slap the label on China (it still might be tough for Europe, since the \uc2 ECB\uc2  doesn't directly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.)\par \par \uc2 If Treasury made that move, the question would then be: so what? The reason for designating a country a\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator is to justify some sort of retaliation. But the Trump administration has already shown both its ability and willingness to use other trade laws to impose sweeping penalties against China and Europe for policies the president says treat the U.S. unfairly.\par \par \uc2 This year the U.S. has imposed three significant rounds of tariffs on China -- first on washing machines and solar panels, second on steel and aluminum, and third on about $34 billion of intermediate goods imported each year. The White House has said it would assess tariffs on a further $200 billion in Chinese goods and Mr. Trump has at times threatened to apply tariffs on all U.S. imports from China.\par \par \uc2 The administration also has placed tariffs on steel and aluminum from the \uc2 EU\uc2 , and is moving forward with a process that would allow tariffs on automobiles and auto parts -- a huge part of EU-U.S. trade.\par \par \uc2 By the standards outlined by Mr. Trump's Treasury Department, China and the \uc2 EU\uc2  aren't \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulators. But that might be little comfort in Beijing and Brussels since they're facing significant tariffs anyway.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 25-07-18 0551GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020180725ee7p0005w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
