\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Trading: Volatile Yuan Signals Worry About Trade -- WSJ\b0\par\par\uc2 858 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Juli 2018\par 08:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par B13\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 The growing unease between the U.S. and China is rattling financial markets \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par By Saumya Vaishampayan \f28 \par \par \uc2 This article is being republished as part of our daily reproduction of WSJ.com articles that also appeared in the U.S. print edition of The Wall Street Journal (July 21, 2018).\par \par \uc2 The yuan took its largest one-day tumble in two years Friday before recovering, the latest sign that investors are struggling to get a grip on the trade clash between the U.S. and China and the uncertainty it is creating in financial markets.\par \par \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  set the yuan 0.9% lower against the dollar early Friday, the largest decline in the so-called daily fixing since 2016. The yuan's decline extended a three-month pullback, a shift drawing attention on Wall Street due to concerns that China could unsettle markets and inflame tensions if it chooses to sharply weaken its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to help counteract tariffs.\par \par \uc2 But the yuan snapped back Friday, posting gains that left it up 0.2% against the dollar, after President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  lashed out at the strength of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's plans to raise U.S. interest rates.\par \par \uc2 Investors said the latest remarks add to the growing unease in markets over the U.S.-China relationship. The standoff figures to increase volatility in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  and interest-rate markets.\par \par \uc2 "Fermenting all this uncertainty certainly goes against the goals of the Trump administration," said \uc2 Eswar Prasad\uc2 , a trade professor at Cornell University. "It creates more uncertainty in the global trading environment, and all that uncertainty ends up...putting more upward pressure on the dollar."\par \par \uc2 At the heart of Mr. Trump's complaints is that the strength of the dollar helps other countries by making U.S. goods more expensive compared to China and European exports.\par \par \uc2 During a CNBC interview Thursday, Mr. Trump said the stronger dollar "puts us at a disadvantage." On Friday morning, he said on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 : "China, the \uc2 European Union\uc2  and others have been manipulating their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 The remarks echo comments Mr. Trump and other administration officials have made on the strength of the dollar over the past two years. In January 2017, before Mr. Trump took office, he told The Wall Street Journal: "Our companies can't compete with [China] now because our \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is too strong. And it's killing us."\par \par \uc2 The unease comes even as policy and economic factors, rather than political threats, seem to be the main drivers of both the dollar and yuan.\par \par \uc2 In the past month, the yuan has fallen 4.5% against the dollar in mainland trading and 3.5% against a broader basket of its trading partners' \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to a Wind Info index. The dollar, meanwhile, closed at its highest level in more than a year on Thursday before a 0.7% decline on Friday.\par \par \uc2 The yuan selloff has been spurred by signs of slowing growth that would argue for a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the U.S. economy likely just wrapped up its strongest quarter of growth in years, encouraging the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  to raise interest rates and helping drive up the value of the dollar.\par \par \uc2 "It is difficult to successfully weaken the dollar through jawboning," said \uc2 Brad Setser\uc2 , a senior fellow at \uc2 the Council on Foreign Relations\uc2 . "The administration is between a fiscal policy that supports a strong dollar and trade action that is pushing China to allow its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken."\par \par \uc2 Many analysts and investors say Beijing has been content to see the yuan weaken in line with market prices rather than actively depressing its value -- provided the moves aren't violent enough to spur panic. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  determines a daily exchange rate, known as the fix, based on the previous day's close and allows trading as much as 2% above or below that level in mainland China.\par \par \uc2 Yu Yongding, a former adviser to the PBOC, said Beijing was aware a weaker yuan would help the economy amid a potential trade war, but added: "The devaluation is the result of market forces."\par \par \uc2 Beijing, however, has previously been reluctant to let its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciate too quickly. A modest devaluation in August 2015 sparked fears about a slowdown in the world's second-largest economy, sending global stock and commodity prices tumbling.\par \par \uc2 Authorities struggled to control subsequent capital outflows, burning through nearly $1 trillion in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves in 17 months to control the pace of depreciation.\par \par \uc2 During Asian trading hours Friday, \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  appeared to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in markets during to help support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts said.\par \par \uc2 " Beijing is intending to send a signal to the U.S. with the recent [yuan] move. At the same time, there are potentially high costs associated with a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war for Beijing," said Robin Brooks, chief economist at the Institute of International Finance.\par \par \uc2 "China has its own constraints, which are material and which -- in my opinion -- should prevent an all-out \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war," Mr. Brooks added\par \par \uc2 Write to Saumya Vaishampayan at saumya.vaishampayan@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 July 21, 2018 02:32 ET (06:32 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020180721ee7l00071\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
