\par\par \b \uc2 MORNING BID EUROPE-Nessun Dorma Trump fired up ahead of Putin summit\b0\par\par\uc2 985 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Juli 2018\par 09:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2018 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * A look at the day ahead from EMEA Head of Desk Jon Boyle and EMEA Deputy Markets Editor Sujata Rao. The views expressed are their own.\par \par \uc2 LONDON, July 16 (Reuters) - It's as if the sun never goes down on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  's tweet account. From the land of the midnight sun, POTUS was blaming everyone but himself early on Monday for the parlous state of U.S.-Russia relations. He holds his first-ever summit with Russian counterpart \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  in Helsinki later in the day, and it's anyone's guess what will actually come out of the meeting. Putin would clearly like a deal to restore full diplomatic staff levels after a round of mutual expulsions over the poisoning of a former Russian spy in Britain. After a tumultuous \uc2 NATO\uc2  summit last week in which they were roundly criticised by the U.S. president, America's European allies would see any move to weaken sanctions on Russian over its annexation of Ukraine as a further disavowal. The nuclear arms race and Syria could also feature on the largely unscripted agenda. With both sides studiously lowering expectations about concrete outcomes, maybe some sort or surprise is being prepared. Trump, after all, sees himself as the consummate deal-maker. Meanwhile, back in Brexit Britain, parliament will vote on the so-called customs bill, which has become the lightening rod for attacks by eurosceptics lawmakers in Prime Minister \uc2 Theresa May\uc2  's governing Conservative party. They want to harden Britain's stance in divorce negotiations with the \uc2 European Union\uc2  . Former Conservative minister Justine Greening has called for a new referendum on the final Brexit deal, to take the decision out of the hands of "deadlocked politicians".\par \par \uc2 MARKETS\par \par \uc2 World stocks closed last week with a second week of gains, led up by the SPX at five-month highs, but it\u8217\'20\'19s a less auspicious start this morning. Data showed China\u8217\'20\'19s second-quarter economic expansion was the slowest since 2016 and factory output faltered as the government\u8217\'20\'19s crackdown on debt took effect. It\u8217\'20\'19s early to see what effect the trade war might have on China, but clearly momentum is slowing and that could cast a pall world growth, one of the things that\u8217\'20\'19s keeping the cycle going. With mainland shares down 0.8 percent, the effect filtered into Asian markets, including Australia, and Asian shares fell some 0.5 percent. But equities are still seeing some support from U.S. earnings growth, which is forecast at 20 percent-plus. European stocks have opened mostly flat, but Wall Street futures are in positive territory.\par \par \uc2 Data today include U.S. retail sales for June and euro zone trade. It is also a presidents\u8217\'20\'19 day of sorts -- we have Trump meeting Putin in Helsinki, and Chinese President \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  hosting European Council President Donald Tusk and European Commission President \uc2 Jean-Claude Juncker\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 On the earnings front, the "N" of FAANGs, \uc2 Netflix\uc2  , reports results. So do \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  (following banking peers Citi and \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2  last week) and \uc2 BlackRock\uc2  , the world\u8217\'20\'19s biggest asset manager. \uc2 Netflix\uc2  shares sank 4 percent on Friday on expectations that subscriber growth numbers may disappoint.\par \par \uc2 China\u8217\'20\'19s yuan last week posted a fifth straight week of losses but has received some respite today as the dollar index eased to five-day lows. The euro has posted modest gains after China pledged the \uc2 European Union\uc2  would get improved market access . China- and trade-focused \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the Aussie have also been lifted. The British pound suffered its worst week in two months and this morning it\u8217\'20\'19s flat, before parliament starts to debate \uc2 Theresa May\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s Brexit plans this afternoon. Meanwhile, the yield curve on U.S. Treasuries remains stuck at the flattest in 11 years, with the spread between two-year and 10-year notes at 24.6 basis points. Oil has eased, with Brent standing around $75.\par \par \uc2 In European markets, the damage from China\u8217\'20\'19s weaker data is putting further pressure on a market already bruised by the Sino-U.S. trade war. Stocks have opened mixed, with Germany\u8217\'20\'19s DAX flat, resisting losses despite being the most China-exposed of Europe\u8217\'20\'19s equity indexes Investor attention will begin to turn to Europe\u8217\'20\'19s earnings season starting in earnest this week.\par \par \uc2 In company news, \uc2 Indivior\uc2  has jumped 25 percent after the drug company won a preliminary injunction against generic rival \uc2 Dr Reddy\u8217\'20\'19s Laboratories\uc2  . Chipmaker Dialog Semiconductor is up 2 percent after it upped its margin expectations in results reported after the close on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Emerging markets stocks snapped a two-day winning streak to fall 0.4 percent. Chinese mainland stocks \u8211\'20\'13 already the two worst performing major EM indexes this year \u8211\'20\'13 fell some 0.9 percent. But \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  made some headway against a softer dollar, with South Africa\u8217\'20\'19s rand clocking the biggest gains, strengthening 0.8 percent. Russia\u8217\'20\'19s rouble and Turkey\u8217\'20\'19s lira gained 0. 3 percent; the latter shrugged off Fitch's downgrade of Turkey on Friday deeper into junk territory, citing a widening current account deficit, a jump in inflation and the wider fallout from the plunging lira. China\u8217\'20\'19s yuan reversed earlier losses to strengthen 0.1 percent. Pakistani\u8217\'20\'19s rupee dropped sharply on Monday, in what appeared to be another devaluation by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which would be its fourth such \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since early December.\par \par \uc2 Events/data/themes on Monday:\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  Q2 GDP, June industrial output, retail sales and urban investment\par \par \uc2 E-China summit in Beijing\par \par \uc2 Indonesia June trade\par \par \uc2 Europe corp events: Alfa Laval, Galp, \uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2  (sales)\par \par \uc2 EZ/Italy May trade\par \par \uc2 Netherlands DTC government bond auction\par \par \uc2 Czech May current account\par \par \uc2 Israel Q1 GDP, June inflation\par \par \uc2 US Q2 earnings: \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  , \uc2 Netflix\uc2  , Blackrock\par \par \uc2 US June retail sales, May inventories (Editing by \uc2 Larry King\uc2 ) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2018-7-16T08:53:30.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020180716ee7g00b1f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
