\par\par \b \uc2 Chinese Yuan Steadies After Large Drop in Official Rate\b0\par\par\uc2 By Saumya Vaishampayan \par \uc2 1,019 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Juli 2018\par 14:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 The Chinese yuan recovered from a morning decline to end roughly flat against the dollar on Thursday, after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  guided the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to its largest one-day drop against its U.S. counterpart in a year and a half.\par \par \uc2 The People's Bank of China\uc2  earlier weakened the yuan by 0.7% to 6.6726 per dollar. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  determines a daily dollar-yuan exchange rate, known as the fix, and allows the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pair to trade as much as 2% above or below that level.\par \par \uc2 The change in the fix echoed market trading the previous day, but was less dramatic than many analysts had expected. Traders had pushed the yuan down after the U.S. unveiled tariffs on $200 billion more of Chinese goods.\par \par \uc2 The yuan fell further on Thursday morning before rebounding to close little changed at 6.6679 per dollar at 4:30 p.m. It was slightly weaker in the offshore market in Hong Kong, where trading ranges or hours aren't restricted, trading at 6.6868.\par \par \uc2 Mainland share indexes also advanced, suggesting a broader recovery in investor sentiment could have helped, rather than any \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Still, while a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  makes \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  more competitive, it is in Beijing's interest to control the pace of any decline to avoid sparking capital outflows.\par \par \uc2 "This is very different from 2015, when it was the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  who initiated the move," said Eddie Cheung, Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Standard Chartered Bank , referring to a previous devaluation. "This time around, it's driven by the market," Cheung said. "The \uc2 PBOC\uc2  has been leaning against all of this."\par \par \uc2 An intensifying trade conflict between the world's two largest economies has put fresh pressure on the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent weeks. Monetary policy is also weighing it down: while \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could cut interest rates or free up banks to lend more in the coming months, the U.S. is set to keep raising rates.\par \par \uc2 The yuan has now declined 2.3% against the dollar in 2018, retracing gains made early in the year. It has also reversed its annual advance against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  weighted by trade volumes, according to a gauge published by Wind Info.\par \par \uc2 Tracy Chen, a portfolio manager at Brandywine Global, said Chinese authorities had earlier tried to use the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  "as a gesture to show the U.S. that they didn't want to have a trade war." She said she viewed its recent decline "as a catch-up depreciation."\par \par \uc2 Write to Saumya Vaishampayan at saumya.vaishampayan@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 12 Jul 2018 16:45 ET  Chinese Yuan Rebounds -- Update\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par By Saumya Vaishampayan and Chelsey Dulaney \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Chinese yuan rallied against the dollar on Thursday, but investors remain on edge as the country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  continues to allow the yuan to weaken amid an intensifying trade fight with the U.S.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Chinese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rose 0.6% against the dollar in offshore markets during Thursday's U.S. trading session, extending a rebound that began in Asian trade. That came after the \f28 \uc2 \f2 People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  guided the yuan down by 0.7% at its daily fixing, the largest-one day decline in 18 months.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Onshore, where the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is allowed to trade as much as 2% above or below the daily fix, the yuan ended little changed against the dollar.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Despite Thursday's reprieve, investors are still on edge over the yuan's rapid descent over the past month, which has spurred fears that China is using a weakened \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as a form of retaliation against the U.S.'s trade threats.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yuan has now declined more than 2% against the dollar in 2018, retracing gains made early in the year. It has also reversed its annual advance against a basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  weighted by trade volumes, according to a gauge published by Wind Info.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A weaker \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would make China's exports cheaper and could help cushion the impact of U.S. tariffs on China's economy, which has already been slowing. On Tuesday, the U.S. said it would impose 10% tariffs on $200 billion in Chinese products, less than a week after the countries slapped tariffs on $34 billion in each other's goods.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  depreciation would be a risky move for China. In 2015, an unexpected devaluation in China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sparked capital flight as Chinese companies, citizens and investors sought to escape further declines. Allowing the yuan to weaken too fast could revive those outflows, adding further downward pressure on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  that economists warn could become difficult for Beijing to manage.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Volatility in China's markets has also proven destabilizing to global markets in recent years because of the country's outsize role in the global economy. Many U.S. companies have large businesses in China, and weakening growth there has sometimes weighed on revenues of companies like \f28 \uc2 \f2 Caterpillar Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2  .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China also is one of the largest users of commodities such as coal and iron ore; softer demand for those products has rattled both those markets and the economies of the countries that export them.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 So far, analysts see few signs that China is aiming for a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  devaluation.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In addition to the trade-related worries that have driven investors to dump the yuan, monetary policy is also weighing the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  down. While \f28 \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could cut interest rates or free up banks to lend more in the coming months, the U.S. is set to keep raising rates.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "This is very different from 2015, when it was the \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  who initiated the move," said Eddie Cheung, Asia \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at Standard Chartered Bank , referring to a previous devaluation.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "This time around, it's driven by the market," Mr. Cheung said. "The \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  has been leaning against all of this."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Write to Saumya Vaishampayan at saumya.vaishampayan@wsj.com and Chelsey Dulaney at Chelsey.Dulaney@wsj.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 July 12, 2018 16:45 ET (20:45 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020180712ee7c0017w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
