\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 3-China's  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  reserves post surprise gain, withstand June market chaos\b0\par\par\uc2 697 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Juli 2018\par 06:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2018 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * China June \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves rose $1.51 bln vs $14.23 bln fall in May               \par \par \uc2 * Small increase due to asset price changes - SAFE               \par \par \uc2 * Reserves supported by gains in U.S. Treasuries - analysts               \par \par \uc2 * Chinese yuan suffered worst month on record in June, stocks worst in two years               \par \par \uc2 * SAFE says \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  expected to remain stable (Adds pictures, graphic slug)               \par \par \uc2 By Yawen Chen and Kevin Yao               \par \par \uc2 BEIJING, July 9 (Reuters) - China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves unexpectedly rose in June, bolstered by an increase in the value of its \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  holdings in an otherwise volatile month for Chinese markets, which were battered by fears about a Sino-U.S. trade war.               \par \par \uc2 Reserves rose $1.51 billion in June to $3.112 trillion, compared with a drop of $14.23 billion in May, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data showed on Monday. Economists polled by Reuters had expected reserves to drop by $10.6 billion to $3.10 trillion.               \par \par \uc2 China's State Administration of \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  (SAFE) said in a statement the small increase in reserves was due to asset price changes but did not provide details.               \par \par \uc2 Analysts pointed to the performance of U.S. bonds in June, which are believed to make up a major part of China's reserves.               \par \par \uc2 "\uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yields were down in June, so valuation factors were conducive to an increase in foreign reserves," said Er Yongjian, chief financial analyst at Bank of Communications.               \par \par \uc2 Fears about a global trade war were among the factors in June that drove investment flows into safe haven assets, such as \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  bonds.               \par \par \uc2 China is the largest holder of \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  debt. Its holdings fell to $1.182 trillion in April from $1.188 trillion in May, data from the \uc2 U.S. Treasury Department\uc2  showed. China also invests its reserves in other U.S. instruments as well as sovereign debt of other countries.               \par \par \uc2 At the same time, the U.S. dollar index rose slightly by 0.7 percent in June, compared with a sharper gain of 2.3 percent in May, according to \uc2 Thomson Reuters\uc2  data.               \par \par \uc2 "The impact of a strengthening U.S. dollar on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves in June was not as big as in May," said Wen Bin, chief analyst at \uc2 China Minsheng Bank\uc2 .               \par \par \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and equity markets had been on edge ahead of July 6, when U.S. tariffs on $34 billion worth of Chinese goods kicked in. Beijing has retaliated with tariffs on U.S. products of the same value.               \par \par \uc2 The heightened Sino-U.S. trade tensions have sparked concerns of capital outflows from China and threatened to pile more pressure on the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .               \par \par \uc2 In June, the yuan suffered its worst month on record, falling 3.3 percent against the U.S. dollar. June also represented the worst month for Chinese stocks in more than two years.               \par \par \uc2 Capital flight was seen as a major risk for China at the start of 2017, but a combination of tighter capital controls and a faltering dollar helped the yuan stage a strong turnaround, bolstering confidence in the economy.               \par \par \uc2 Last year, the yuan reversed three straight years of depreciation against the U.S. dollar and China's cross-border capital flows went from net outflows to basically stable.               \par \par \uc2 The yuan fell through a key psychological level of 6.7 on the U.S. dollar on July 3. The Chinese authorities made remarks the same day to assure markets it would keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stable.               \par \par \uc2 Market participants last week said major state-owned banks were seen swapping yuan for dollars in the forwards market and immediately selling them into the spot market to prop up the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .               \par \par \uc2 Despite the market tumult in recent weeks, \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  appears broadly comfortable with a weakening yuan and would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  only to prevent any destabilizing declines or to restore market confidence, policy insiders told Reuters.               \par \par \uc2 Authorities are also confident they won't have to make heavy use of the official \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to defend the yuan as they did during 2015 when stocks and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  went into a tailspin.               \par \par \uc2 Released: 2018-7-9T05:50:10.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020180709ee79003ml\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
