\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Full-throttle car rally drives back trade gloom\b0\par\par\uc2 1,024 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Juli 2018\par 14:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2018 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * European car shares jump on report on U.S. tariff compromise\par \par \uc2 * German data, \uc2 ECB\uc2  report lifts euro, bond yields\par \par \uc2 * Oil rises, bucking Trump's \uc2 Twitter\uc2  demands for \uc2 OPEC\uc2  action\par \par \uc2 * Markets await U.S. tariffs on China, Fed minutes\par \par \uc2 * Yuan slips back\par \par \uc2 * Graphic: World \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates in 2018 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://tmsnrt.rs/2egbfVh"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://tmsnrt.rs/2egbfVh}}}\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 By Marc Jones\par \par \uc2 LONDON, July 5 (Reuters) - European shares headed for a third day of gains on Thursday as reassuring economic data from Germany and a report that its big carmakers could be spared from U.S. tariffs offset another gloomy session for Asia.\par \par \uc2 Trade nerves had pegged Asia's bourses back with a U.S. deadline to impose tariffs on Chinese imports just a day away, but it was another widely-flagged target - the German car sector - that spurred Europe's rally and pushed up Wall Street futures.\par \par \uc2 Mercedes-maker Daimler, BMW Porsche and \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  raced up as much as 5 percent after reports of a U.S. offer to suspend tariff threats on EU-made cars if the bloc lifts its duties on U.S. ones.\par \par \uc2 It fuelled the wider European auto sector's best day in more than two years with the mood also been helped by a stronger-than-expected jump in German industrial orders after four months of falls.\par \par \uc2 "With these stories coming out (about car tariffs), you have a sector which has been very oversold meeting some potential good news on the trade war front," said Bank of America Merrill Lynch European equity strategist James Barty.\par \par \uc2 "This is going to be the dominant issue of the summer. Are we heading for a full-blown trade war in which case it is very bad news for risk assets, or do we walk away from it, in which case, as we have seen today, markets are likely to rebound quite sharply."\par \par \uc2 The euro briefly topped $1.17 and bond yields rose after the brighter German data and after a report that the \uc2 ECB\uc2  thinks markets are now too cautious on when it will raise euro zone interest rates next year.\par \par \uc2 Sterling gained ground too as the head of the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  confirmed it was still planning to raise UK rates this year. Later in the day, traders will get the minutes from last month's \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  meeting when it raised U.S. rates for a second time this year.\par \par \uc2 "The euro is getting a bit of a lift on the German data though the trade concerns will continue to dominate markets with the Fed minutes being the key data point," said Kenneth Broux, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  in London.\par \par \uc2 ASIAN ANGST\par \par \uc2 The ongoing angst over U.S. President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's trade tariffs had extended the recent slide in Asian markets overnight, in particular Chinese shares which are now deep into "bear" market territory.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, U.S. tariffs on $34 billion worth of Chinese imports will take effect. Beijing has promised to retaliate in kind, though it said overnight that it would "absolutely not" fire the first shot in a trade war.\par \par \uc2 MSCI's broadest index of Asia-Pacific shares outside Japan , which has dropped every day except three since mid June, ended down 0.25 percent. The index has lost about 2 percent this week, plumbing a nine-month low in the process.\par \par \uc2 Japan's Nikkei shed 0.8 percent, South Korea's KOSPI slipped 0.4 percent, Hong Kong's Hang Seng was off 0.2 percent and the Shanghai Composite Index fell 0.9 percent to take its dive since late January to 23 percent.\par \par \uc2 With Europe advancing though, Wall Street's S&P 500 futures and Dow and \uc2 Nasdaq\uc2  futures were all up 0.6-0.7 percent, pointing to a solid start following Wednesday's U.S. Independence Day holiday.\par \par \uc2 "The $34 billion U.S. tariffs figure has been mostly factored by the markets and focus is now on what the United States says on the remaining $16 billion (of a promised $50 billion tariff plan)," said Masahiro Ichikawa, senior strategist at Sumitomo Mitsui Asset Management in Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 WEAPONIZE\par \par \uc2 In \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the euro's gain was the dollar's loss. That also helped ease some of the pressure on volatile emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , even Turkey's battered lira recovered from an early slip triggered by its prime minister saying cutting interest rates was a top priority.\par \par \uc2 The Chinese yuan was slightly lower though, its recovery from an 11-month low stalling. A rebound was triggered in the past two sessions after the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sought to calm nervous markets and stem the recent tumble.\par \par \uc2 The longer term direction for the yuan was still unclear. \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  appears broadly comfortable with a weakening yuan and would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  only to prevent any destabilising declines or to restore market confidence, policy insiders told Reuters.\par \par \uc2 "Although we do not believe China will weaponize its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , we do believe the current trajectory of Chinese yuan depreciation is justified," strategists at \uc2 Bank of America Merrill Lynch\uc2  wrote in a note.\par \par \uc2 The dollar was 0.1 percent higher against the yen at 110.610 yen, though it was sticking within a narrow range ahead of the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  minutes that could give fresh clues on \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rate hike plans.\par \par \uc2 In commodities, Brent oil futures were on the rise again at $78.30 a barrel, a day after U.S. President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  sent a tweet demanding that \uc2 OPEC\uc2  reduce prices for crude.\par \par \uc2 Brent had risen on Wednesday on a threat from an Iranian commander to disrupt oil shipments from neighbouring states if Washington continued to press all countries to stop buying Iranian oil, and a drop in U.S. crude inventories.\par \par \uc2 "If Trump continues to believe that \uc2 OPEC\uc2  are not doing enough, we would not rule out an SPR (Strategic Petroleum Reserve) release from the U.S., or possibly even export restrictions on petroleum products," ING said in a note.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Saikat Chatterjee, Dmitry Zhdannikov and Shadia Nasralla in London Editing by Mark Heinrich) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2018-7-5T13:07:15.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020180705ee7500gwi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
