\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Asian equities extend losses on eve of U.S. tariff deadline\b0\par\par\uc2 825 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Juli 2018\par 07:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2018 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * MSCI Asia-Pacific index down 0.5 pct, Nikkei loses 1 pct\par \par \uc2 * Spreadbetters expect European stocks to open little changed\par \par \uc2 * Moves limited before Washington's July 6 tariffs deadline\par \par \uc2 * Trade woes keep copper, zinc stuck near one-year lows\par \par \uc2 * Crude slips after Trump demands \uc2 OPEC\uc2  "reduce pricing now"\par \par \uc2 By Shinichi Saoshiro\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, July 5 (Reuters) - Asian stocks fell for the fourth day and major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  traded in tight ranges on Thursday, with financial markets jittery before a U.S. deadline to impose tariffs on Chinese imports just a day away.\par \par \uc2 Spreadbetters expected European stocks to open little changed, with Britain's FTSE flat, Germany's DAX 0.02 percent lower and France's CAC down 0.1 percent.\par \par \uc2 MSCI's broadest index of Asia-Pacific shares outside Japan , which has been drifting lower since Monday, was down 0.5 percent. The index has lost about 2 percent this week, during which it plumbed a nine-month low.\par \par \uc2 S&P 500 futures edged up 0.1 percent while Dow futures lost 0.1 percent, pointing to a mixed start for Wall Street later in the day when trading resumes following Wednesday's Independence Day holiday.\par \par \uc2 Japan's Nikkei lost 1 percent, South Korea's KOSPI slipped 0.75 percent, Hong Kong's Hang Seng was off 0.9 percent and the Shanghai Composite Index fell 0.9 percent.\par \par \uc2 "The markets lack strong direction without incentives from the United States, where their markets were closed yesterday. Moves by Chinese shares and the yuan remain a key factor in the meantime," said Masahiro Ichikawa, senior strategist at Sumitomo Mitsui Asset Management in Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 The United States plans to implement tariffs on $50 billion worth of imports from China as both nations remained locked in a bitter trade dispute that has convulsed global financial markets in recent weeks.\par \par \uc2 On July 6, tariffs on $34 billion worth of imports will take effect, and Beijing has promised to retaliate in kind.\par \par \uc2 However, \uc2 China's finance ministry\uc2  did say on Wednesday that it will "absolutely not" fire the first shot in a trade war with the United States and will not be the first to levy tariffs.\par \par \uc2 China has put pressure on the \uc2 European Union\uc2  to issue a strong joint statement against U.S. President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's trade policies, European officials told Reuters..\par \par \uc2 "The $34 billion U.S. tariffs figure has been mostly factored by the markets and focus is now on what the United States says on the remaining $16 billion," Ichikawa said.\par \par \uc2 In \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the euro was little changed at $1.1655 . The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell to as low as $1.1630 overnight after weaker confidence in the euro zone overshadowed better-than-expected data on business activity.\par \par \uc2 The Chinese yuan was slightly lower, its recovery from an 11-month low stalling. A rebound in the yuan was triggered in the past two sessions after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sought to calm nervous markets midweek and stem the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent tumble.\par \par \uc2 The yuan was a touch lower after gaining 0.2 percent against the dollar on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The longer term direction for the yuan was still unclear. \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  appears broadly comfortable with a weakening yuan and would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  only to prevent any destabilising declines or to restore market confidence, policy insiders told Reuters.\par \par \uc2 "The July 6 deadline for the imposition of U.S. tariffs on China imports looms large. Although we do not believe China will weaponize its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , we do believe the current trajectory of Chinese yuan depreciation is justified," strategists at \uc2 Bank of America Merrill Lynch\uc2  wrote in a note.\par \par \uc2 The dollar was 0.1 percent lower at 110.365 yen, trapped in a narrow range the previous day due to the U.S. holiday, awaiting minutes from the June Federal Reserve policy meeting due later in the day for immediate cues.\par \par \uc2 The dollar index against a basket of six major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  was 0.15 percent lower at 94.553.\par \par \uc2 Copper and zinc were stuck near one-year lows, having declined steadily over the past month on fears that the U.S.-China trade spat could reduce demand for industrial metals.\par \par \uc2 Copper on the \uc2 London Metal Exchange\uc2  (\uc2 LME\uc2 ) traded at $6,391.50 per tonne after slumping to a low of $6,344 the previous day, its weakest since August 2017.\par \par \uc2 LME\uc2  zinc was at $2,695 a tonne after dropping to $2,687.50 overnight, the lowest since June last year.\par \par \uc2 Brent crude futures were down 0.7 percent at $77.69 a barrel, losing some steam after two successive days of gains after U.S. President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  sent a tweet demanding that \uc2 OPEC\uc2  reduce prices for crude.\par \par \uc2 Brent had risen on Wednesday on a threat from an Iranian commander and a drop in U.S. crude inventories. (Editing by Shri Navaratnam and Jacqueline Wong) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2018-7-5T06:19:10.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020180705ee75006sh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
