\par\par \b \uc2 Asian Morning Briefing: U.S. Stocks End Lower\b0\par\par\uc2 1,984 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Juli 2018\par 23:37\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par MARKETS AT A GLANCE \par (Data as of approximately 5 p.m. ET) \par  \par                          LAST    CHANGE   % CHG \par DJIA                   24174.82 -132.36  -0.54% \par Nasdaq                   7502.67  -65.01  -0.86% \par S&P 500                 2713.22  -13.49  -0.49% \par Japan: Nikkei 225      21785.54  -26.39  -0.12% \par Hang Seng              28545.57 -409.54  -1.41% \par Shanghai Composite      2786.89   11.33   0.41% \par S&P BSE  Sensex         35378.60  114.19   0.32% \par Australia: S&P/ASX      6210.20   32.40   0.52% \par UK: FTSE 100            7593.29   45.44   0.60% \par  \par                  PRICE CHG  YIELD \par U.S. 2 Year          2/32   2.528 \par U.S. 5 Year          4/32   2.725 \par U.S. 10 Year        11/32   2.829 \par Australia 10 Year   -5/32   2.624 \par China 10 Year       -8/32   3.531 \par India 10 Year        0/32   7.886 \par Japan 10 Year       -1/32   0.029 \par German 10 Year       3/32   0.295 \par  \par                         LAST(MID)  CHANGE \par Australia $ (AUD/USD)     0.7387   0.0001 \par Yen (USD/JPY)             110.57    -0.02 \par S. Korean Won (USD/KRW)  1114.96    -0.03 \par Chinese Yuan (USD/CNY)    6.6422   0.0002 \par Euro (EUR/USD)            1.1659  -0.0002 \par WSJ Dollar Index           88.09     0.01 \par  \par                LAST   CHANGE  % CHG \par Crude Oil      74.28    0.34  0.46% \par Brent Crude    77.89    0.59  0.76% \par Gold          1254.0    12.3  0.99% \par  \par SNAPSHOT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stocks closed lower in a holiday-shortened trading session. \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. government\f28 \uc2 \f2  bond prices rose as investors sold equities. The dollar was lower after resolution of a political squabble in Germany. Crude oil ended lower in a volatile session. And gold was sharply higher. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par OPENING CALL \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hong Kong's retail sector logged a fourth-straight month of double-digit sales growth in May, helped by what the city's government calls "sanguine" local-consumption sentiment and a "visible increase" in visitor arrivals. May sales were 13% higher than a year earlier, versus April's 12% growth. A government spokesman says the near-term outlook for retail sales remains positive given the city's robust labor market and sustained growth in inbound tourism. Hong Kong's retail sales ended 2 years of declines in March 2017. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EQUITIES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stocks fell in the final hour of trading in a holiday-shortened session, dragged down by shares of technology and financial companies.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Major indexes had traded higher for most of the day but, for the second consecutive session, changed directions shortly ahead of the close. Technology stocks, which have powered the market this year, took a breather after leading Monday's modest charge higher.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average fell 132 points, or 0.5%, to 24175, after earlier rising as much as 138 points. The S&P 500 dropped 0.5%, while the technology-heavy Nasdaq Composite declined 0.9%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The declines came as trading volumes fell to their lowest level since the abbreviated session on Nov. 24, 2017. U.S. stock markets closed at 1 p.m. EDT ahead of the U.S. Independence Day holiday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Five of 11 sectors in the broad S&P 500 declined, with shares of tech companies falling the most at 1.4%. Financial stocks also fell with government bond yields. Energy shares were one of the bright spots in the index, climbing 0.7%, even after U.S. crude prices turned lower after topping $75 a barrel.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A number of Asian stock markets saw an afternoon rebound as Monday's wide selling carried over into the morning.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As there appeared to be a buyer's strike Monday, it looked by midday that the selling had run its course for now. Chinese indexes finished higher, though big caps again lagged, as the market was helped by a bounce in the yuan after the \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBoC\f28 \uc2 \f2 's chief looked to soothe investors.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's Nikkei nearly erased an earlier near-1% drop and Korea's Kospi rebounded 0.5%. But Hong Kong stocks were down upwards of 2% after not partaking in Monday's market festivities due to a holiday and Indonesia's benchmark is off 1.5%. S&P 500 futures are up 0.2%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hong Kong's Hang Seng dropped 1.4%. Weakness in Hong Kong stocks didn't spread to mainland Chinese bourses, however, with the gap widening between the yuan on the mainland -- where the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Chinese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  fixes its daily level -- and in trading hubs like Hong Kong, suggesting a possible souring in sentiment among foreign investors on China-exposed assets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Shanghai Composite Index was up 0.4% after plunging 2.5% to its lowest close since early 2016. \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar fell against the euro, after German Chancellor \f28 \uc2 \f2 Angela Merkel\f28 \uc2 \f2  reached a compromise in a battle over immigration that threatened her government.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro was recently up 0.1% at $1.1643. The WSJ Dollar Index, which measures the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  against a basket of 16 others, was down 0.2% at 88.18.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Political uncertainty and weaker-than-expected economic data have taken their toll on the euro, dragging the common \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  down around 3% against the dollar this year. Ms. Merkel must still persuade her left-center allies to back tighter immigration rules.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hopes that a new Mexican president will be able to move trade negotiations with the U.S. forward powered the peso higher. The dollar was recently down 1.9% against the Mexican \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar fell also against other emerging market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , giving up 0.5% against the Brazilian real and 0.7% against the South African rand. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BONDS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. government\f28 \uc2 \f2  bond prices jumped as investors sold stocks heading into the July 4 holiday, stoking demand for Treasurys.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yield on the benchmark 10-year \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  note settled at 2.833%, compared with 2.867% Monday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Yields, which fall as bond prices rise, rose overnight as global stocks mostly climbed, with some analysts attributing the action to lingering tailwinds from stronger-than-expected manufacturing data Monday. The yield on the 10-year note then slipped as U.S. stocks came under selling pressure, ending near its session low.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Analysts had expected trading across financial markets to be relatively quiet ahead of the Independence Day holiday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 When trading resumes Thursday, analysts say they will be looking at minutes from the Federal Open Market Committee's June meeting, as well as the Labor Department's monthly employment report Friday. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par COMMODITIES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Oil prices reversed gains after hitting $75 a barrel, as Saudi Arabia said it is prepared to pump more oil to ease supply constraints in the market.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Prices swung wildly. Light, sweet crude for August delivery traded in the morning at the highest level since November 2014, before reversing course and falling more than 1%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 More recently, it pared losses to be down 0.1% at $73.89 a barrel on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 New York Mercantile Exchange\f28 \uc2 \f2  . Brent, the global benchmark, fell 0.1% to $77.26.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Supply disruptions have lifted prices to multiyear highs this year, and U.S. futures have rallied recently on supply outages in Libya and at a Canadian facility that weighed on available U.S. crude supply. However, the possibility of more supply being added to the market helped ease concerns.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In a statement, Saudi Arabia asserted its willingness to use spare production capacity to increase market supply. Over the weekend, U.S. President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Donald Trump\f28 \uc2 \f2  called on the country to increase production to help ease fast-rising crude prices.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gold futures finished sharply higher, clawing back from a steady decline that had pushed the commodity early this week to its lowest finish since December, as a brawny dollar paused its advance.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Industrial metals, meanwhile, remained in focus, amid trade friction and questions about China's economic expansion. Platinum prices posted a rebound after marking their lowest settlement since late 2008. Copper prices extended losses to a fifth straight session.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 August gold rose $11.80, or nearly 1%, to settle at $1,253.50 an ounce. The contract shed 1% to settle at $1,241.70 Monday. With the stuttering start this week, August gold futures were over 5% so far in 2018. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TODAY'S HEADLINES \par Barnes & Noble Fires CEO Over Policy Violations \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Barnes & Noble\f28 \uc2 \f2  fired CEO Demos Parneros, citing violations of its policies. The company said he won't receive any severance. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par U.S. Auto Sales Remain Strong, but Tariffs Could Squash Momentum \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. auto sales have proved resilient so far in 2018, with many of the largest car sellers posting increases over the first six months of the year despite predictions that demand would cool. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 United Microelectronics\f28 \uc2 \f2  : Chinese Court Issues Injunction Against Micron \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 United Microelectronics\f28 \uc2 \f2  said a Chinese court issued a preliminary injunction against two \f28 \uc2 \f2 Micron Technology\f28 \uc2 \f2  businesses, blocking the product, sale and import of several products. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Billionaire Behind LaCroix Accused of Improper Touching by Two Pilots \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Two pilots have accused Nick A. Caporella, the 82-year-old chief of LaCroix sparkling water, of inappropriately touching them while they were in the cockpit of his business jet. The CEO's lawyer says the allegations are false. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 WPP\f28 \uc2 \f2   and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Martin Sorrell\f28 \uc2 \f2   Submit Rival Bids for Digital Creative Agency \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Ad giant \f28 \uc2 \f2 WPP\f28 \uc2 \f2  and its former CEO \f28 \uc2 \f2 Martin Sorrell\f28 \uc2 \f2  are already competing against each other. The ad holding company submitted an offer for Netherlands-based digital creative agency MediaMonks. A rival bid was put in by Mr. Sorrell's new venture, \f28 \uc2 \f2 S4 Capital\f28 \uc2 \f2 . \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Glencore\f28 \uc2 \f2  Shares Tumble on Subpoena \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Glencore\f28 \uc2 \f2  stock fell after the mining giant received a subpoena from U.S. authorities related to corruption and money-laundering laws at \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in Congo, Nigeria and Venezuela. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 KPMG\f28 \uc2 \f2   Has Close Ties to Troubled Private Equity Firm \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The unraveling of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Abraaj Group\f28 \uc2 \f2  , a once-highflying champion of emerging-markets investing, is drawing attention to the role played by its auditor, a Middle East affiliate of \f28 \uc2 \f2 KPMG\f28 \uc2 \f2  . \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par New York's Iconic Plaza Hotel Sold to Qatar Fund for $600 Million \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A fund controlled by the Gulf state of Qatar has acquired full ownership of New York's famed Plaza Hotel, featured in "The Great Gatsby" and "North by Northwest," for about $600 million. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par U.S. Trade Tensions Send Yuan Sliding, Challenging China \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 3 Jul 2018 17:37 ET \par Asian Morning Briefing: U.S. Stocks End Lower -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yuan's slide is testing Beijing as it grapples with a looming trade war, putting \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  under pressure to revive the type of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  used in the past. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Riksbank\f28 \uc2 \f2   Confirms It's Ready to Hike Rates \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sweden's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reiterated its intention to start raising its key policy rate in the fourth quarter, but signaled that it is in no rush to end negative repo rates. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par RECENT DJ EXCLUSIVES \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Carl Icahn\f28 \uc2 \f2  Unlikely to Push Dell for a Better Deal \par  \par TODAY'S CALENDAR \par (Times in GMT, followed by country and event) \par Wednesday, July 04, 2018 \par 0030  Singapore    June       Singapore Whole Economy PMI \par 0030  Japan        June       Japan Services PMI \par 0130  Australia    May        International Trade in Goods & Services \par 0130  Australia    May        Retail Trade \par 0145  China        June       China Services PMI \par 0200  Australia    June       VFACTS vehicle sales \par 0745  Italy        June       Italy Services PMI \par 0750  France       June       France Services PMI \par 0755  Germany      June       