\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Stocks edge lower as trade worries overshadow rising oil prices\b0\par\par\uc2 659 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Juli 2018\par 19:32\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2018 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Updates through close of U.S. trading.)\par \par \uc2 By David Randall\par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, July 3 - World stocks edged lower Tuesday despite broad gains in Europe and rising oil prices as markets remained in the grip of trade turbulence.\par \par \uc2 A July 6 deadline is looming for Washington to impose tariffs on $34 billion worth of Chinese goods that Beijing has vowed to match with tariffs on U.S. products. President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  also threatened on Monday to "do something" if the United States was not better treated by the \uc2 World Trade Organization\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Prospects of a full-blown trade war and relentless yuan weakening - it has fallen 5 percent in the past two weeks and is near 11-month lows - reportedly forced \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  into \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  via state-run banks.\par \par \uc2 "It is by far the biggest (yuan loss) I can remember. Prudence suggests it has to be matched across Southeast Asia because of the competitive implications," said \uc2 Bank of New York Mellon\uc2  strategist \uc2 Neil Mellor\uc2 . "It generates a degree of instability in the market simply by virtue of its scale."\par \par \uc2 Among equity markets, Hong Kong dived as much as 3.3 percent to nine-month lows, hit also by U.S. curbs on \uc2 China Mobile\uc2 . Shanghai's bourse hit a two-year trough but closed higher as the yuan recovered.\par \par \uc2 The mood was more cheerful in Europe where a pan-European equity index rose 1 percent, the euro firmed and bond yields climbed after German Chancellor \uc2 Angela Merkel\uc2  struck a migration deal with her Bavarian conservative coalition partners.\par \par \uc2 MSCI's gauge of stocks across the globe shed 0.01 percent.\par \par \uc2 In the United States, benchmark indices closed in the red after trading for much of the day in positive territory during a holiday-shortened session. The Dow Jones Industrial Average fell 132.36 points, or 0.54 percent, to 24,174.82, the S&P 500 lost 13.49 points, or 0.49 percent, to 2,713.22 and the Nasdaq Composite dropped 65.01 points, or 0.86 percent, to 7,502.67.\par \par \uc2 Shares of \uc2 Facebook Inc\uc2  fell 2.3 percent after the Washington Post reported a federal probe on a data breach linked to Cambridge Analytica had been broadened and would include more government agencies. That hit other technology shares as well.\par \par \uc2 "The big driver behind U.S. resilience is that tech has been strong," said Rory McPherson, head of investment strategy at asset manager Psigma. "Expectations are pretty high for the earnings season, with talk of 20 percent earnings growth year-on-year."\par \par \uc2 Energy stocks have been boosted by Brent crude's rise past $78 a barrel, McPherson noted. Europe's tech and energy sectors rose 0.5 and 1 percent respectively .\par \par \uc2 While U.S. growth and company earnings seem unassailable, tit-for-tat tariffs from China and Europe may ultimately prove detrimental for American businesses and jobs.\par \par \uc2 U.S. bond yields rose slightly amid the easier mood but concern about the trade row has helped push the gap between two- and 10-year yields to the narrowest since 2007.\par \par \uc2 "Basically (the flat curve) is saying the underlying growth in the U.S. economy may not be as strong as the high short-term interest rates might warrant," McPherson said.\par \par \uc2 The dollar retreated 0.4 percent against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the easing tensions in Germany helped the euro to gain 0.2 percent against the greenback.\par \par \uc2 Friday's monthly payrolls data should show labor markets remain tight, keeping the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2 's policy tightening on track.\par \par \uc2 "Notwithstanding the trade war concerns, the broader picture is the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  still remains the most hawkish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  among its peers and that should support the dollar for now," said \uc2 Jane Foley\uc2 , senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Rabobank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by David Randall Editing by Nick Zieminski and Susan Thomas) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2018-7-3T18:32:41.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020180703ee7300o64\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
