\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-World stocks rise from 2-1/2 month low, China soothes  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  markets\b0\par\par\uc2 884 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Juli 2018\par 13:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2018 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * Trade war concerns grow as July 6 U.S. tariff deadline nears\par \par \uc2 * Shanghai stocks at 2-yr low, HK down 3 pct before edging up\par \par \uc2 * Chinese yuan slips past 6.7; recovers after reported \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * European shares, euro rise after German compromise on migration\par \par \uc2 * Wall Street set for another firmer session on tech\par \par \uc2 * Turkish lira falls as inflation rises to 14-yr highs (Changes quotes, updates prices throughout)\par \par \uc2 By Sujata Rao\par \par \uc2 LONDON, July 3 (Reuters) - World stocks rose on Tuesday, supported by gains in Europe and three straight days of tech-driven rises in the United States, even though markets across Asia and especially China remained in the grip of trade turbulence.\par \par \uc2 Wall Street was set for another firmer session as investors positioned for strong Silicon Valley earnings before the reporting season starts next week, while European shares also rose after a deal on settling a migration policy row that had threatened Germany's coalition government.\par \par \uc2 But a July 6 deadline is looming for Washington to impose tariffs on $34 billion worth of Chinese goods that Beijing has vowed to match with tariffs on U.S. products. President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  also threatened on Monday to "do something" if the United States was not better treated by the \uc2 World Trade Organisation\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Prospects of a full-blown trade war and relentless yuan weakening -- it has fallen 5 percent in the past two weeks near 11-month lows -- reportedly forced \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  into \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  via state-run banks..\par \par \uc2 A \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  advisor was also quoted as saying authorities did not expect significant yuan depreciation. That helped the yuan reverse earlier losses to move back into positive territory for the day against the dollar, standing 0.3 percent firmer in offshore trade at 6.66 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 The gains filtered through to other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as well as the Australian dollar, considered a proxy for China risk, allowing it to edge off 1-1/2-year lows to the greenback .\par \par \uc2 However, forward markets anticipate the yuan will stay under pressure. It is seen trading around 6.7 per dollar in six months' time, with many reckoning Beijing could use \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakness as a tool to counter U.S. trade barriers.\par \par \uc2 "It is by far the biggest (yuan loss) I can remember. Prudence suggests it has to be matched across Southeast Asia because of the competitive implications," said \uc2 Bank of New York Mellon\uc2  strategist \uc2 Neil Mellor\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "It generates a degree of instability in the market simply by virtue of its scale."\par \par \uc2 Among equities, Hong Kong dived as much as 3.3 percent to nine-month lows, hit also by U.S. curbs on \uc2 China Mobile\uc2  . Shanghai's bourse hit a two-year trough but closed higher as the yuan recovered.\par \par \uc2 The mood was more cheerful in Europe where a pan-European equity index rose one percent, the euro firmed and bond yields climbed after German Chancellor \uc2 Angela Merkel\uc2  struck a migration deal with her Bavarian conservative coalition partners.\par \par \uc2 STRONG U.S. EXPECTATIONS\par \par \uc2 Equity futures for the U.S. S&P500, \uc2 Dow Jones\uc2  and \uc2 Nasdaq\uc2  indexes rose around 0.4 percent, indicating Wall Street's tech-driven rally would continue.\par \par \uc2 Tech shares have been relatively resilient to trade fears - the \uc2 New York Stock Exchange\uc2 's index of 10 tech giants including China's Alibaba has gained over 30 percent this year.\par \par \uc2 "The big driver behind U.S. resilience is that tech has been strong," said Rory McPherson, head of investment strategy at asset manager Psigma. "Expectations are pretty high for the earnings season, with talk of 20 percent earnings growth year-on-year."\par \par \uc2 Energy stocks have been boosted by Brent crude's rise past $78 a barrel, he noted. Europe's tech and energy sectors rose 0.5 and 1 percent respectively .\par \par \uc2 That helped MSCI's world index to rise 0.25 percent, inching further off 2-1/2 month lows hit last week.\par \par \uc2 While U.S. growth and company earnings seem unassailable, tit-for-tat tariffs from China and Europe may ultimately prove detrimental for American businesses and jobs.\par \par \uc2 U.S. bond yields rose slightly amid the easier mood but concern about the trade row has helped push the gap between two- and 10-year yields to the narrowest since 2007.\par \par \uc2 "Basically (the flat curve) is saying the underlying growth in the U.S. economy may not be as strong as the high short-term interest rates might warrant," McPherson said.\par \par \uc2 The dollar retreated 0.4 percent against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the easing tensions in Germany helped the euro to gain 0.2 percent against the greenback.\par \par \uc2 However, many reckon the dollar's three-month winning streak will continue for a while yet. Friday's monthly payrolls data should show labour markets remain tight, keeping the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2 's policy tightening on track.\par \par \uc2 "Notwithstanding the trade war concerns, the broader picture is the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  still remains the most hawkish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  among its peers and that should support the dollar for now," said \uc2 Jane Foley\uc2 , senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Rabobank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Tomo Uetake in Tokyo, Marc Jones and Tricia Wright in London; editing by David Stamp and Jan Harvey) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2018-7-3T12:51:30.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020180703ee7300faj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
