\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 2-China's shares, yuan fall as U.S. tariff deadline looms\b0\par\par\uc2 607 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Juli 2018\par 06:33\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2018 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI, July 2 (Reuters) - Chinese stocks slipped on Monday, handing back some hard-won gains from a bounce late last week as worries mount ahead of a U.S. move to impose $34 billion of tariffs on Chinese exports.               \par \par \uc2 The yuan, fresh off its worst month on record, continued to lose ground against the dollar, trading at around 6.6363 at 0406 GMT from a close of 6.6225 on Friday.               \par \par \uc2 Chinese 10-year treasury futures for September were mostly flat, inching down just 0.05 percent.               \par \par \uc2 Markets are jittery ahead of a July 6 deadline when the United States is due to impose the tariffs on Chinese exports. Beijing is expected to respond with tariffs of its own on U.S. goods.               \par \par \uc2 On Friday, Chinese stocks and the yuan bounced with benchmark share indexes having one of their best days since mid-2016.               \par \par \uc2 Still, June represented the worst month for Chinese stocks in more than two years and the yuan's biggest monthly fall on record.               \par \par \uc2 By the midday trading break on Monday, the blue chip CSI300 index was down 1.4 percent, while the Shanghai Composite Index fell 1.1 percent. Financial stocks led declines, with the CSI financial sub-index off 2.1 percent.               \par \par \uc2 Chen Xiaopeng, an analyst with Sealand Securities, said more pessimism was on the cards for Chinese stock investors with the country's economic outlook hurt by prospects of a full-blown trade war between Beijing and Washington.               \par \par \uc2 "It could take at least several months for the major stock indexes to bottom out," he said.               \par \par \uc2 Uncertainties will reinforce investors' inclination to "huddle together for warmth" in outperforming sectors such as consumers and healthcare, Chen said.               \par \par \uc2 An index tracking healthcare firms is up nearly 20 percent so far this year, while the Shanghai index is down around 15 percent.               \par \par \uc2 Hong Kong's markets were closed on Monday for a public holiday to mark the 21st anniversary of the former British colony's return to Chinese rule.               \par \par \uc2 The yuan fell 3.25 percent against the dollar in June and continued its slide despite a firmer-than-expected midpoint set by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday.               \par \par \uc2 "Investors don't care and spot yuan rates continued weakening," a trader said.               \par \par \uc2 If the yuan's decline intensifies, \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  economists wrote in a note on Monday that the \uc2 Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \uc2 People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 , may step up \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .               \par \par \uc2 "CNY may overshoot with shifting market expectations of policy stance amid higher trade tensions, but we don't expect policymakers to encourage material RMB depreciation. The \uc2 PBOC\uc2  could step up \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  if depreciation risk intensifies," they wrote.               \par \par \uc2 On Friday, the last trading day of the month, ING lowered its yuan forecast to 7 per dollar by the end of the year from a previous forecast of 6.6, citing risks to the policy outlook.               \par \par \uc2 "A weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would, at most, be a shield, safeguarding wider damage from a trade war and the hurdles faced by Chinese companies' operating in the U.S.," it said. ING added it did not see any panic in the market.               \par \par \uc2 ING's shift follows a similar move on June 24 by \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 , which said it expected the yuan to depreciate to 6.8 per dollar by the end of this year and 7.2 by the end of 2019. It had previously forecast 6.4 yuan per dollar in each year.               \par \par \uc2 The depreciation will be "driven by an important change of policy stance from tightening to loosening", it said.               \par \par \uc2 Released: 2018-7-2T05:33:01.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020180702ee72003jx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
