\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Global Markets News of the Day\b0\par\par\uc2 434 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Juni 2018\par 11:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Stocks Struggle for Traction as Trade Worries Persist \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Lingering concerns about trade kept global stocks muted although gains in oil prices boosted shares of oil-and-gas companies. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Trump Signals Easing of Demand for Tools to Limit Chinese Investment \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 President Trump suggested he will ease off his demands for new restrictions on Chinese investment in technology industries, relying instead on a 1988 law being updated by Congress that authorizes the government to review foreign investments for national-security problems. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Oil Climbs as U.S. Threatens Sanctions on Nations Importing Iranian Crude \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. oil prices extended gains, rising more than 3% to $70 a barrel, after a senior State Department official said Washington expects all countries to cut oil imports from Iran to zero by Nov. 4 or risk sanctions. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Eurozone Bank Lending Picks Up \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The recovery in eurozone bank lending continued in May, a trend which should underpin corporate investments in the region. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par China's 'Big Mama' Steps In as Yuan Tumbles Further \par  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appeared to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to stem the yuan's decline, after it had earlier set the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at a six-month low against the U.S. dollar. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par One Asian Stock Market is Resisting the Trade War Selloff \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Most Asian stock indexes were slightly lower as markets opened Wednesday. China's Shanghai Composite and Japan's Nikkei 225 led the pack, with each falling by 0.4%. One outlier? India's resilient stock market. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par When China, U.S. Fight Over Trade, European Stocks Get Hurt \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's main stock benchmark entered bear-market territory Tuesday as trade tensions continued to reverberate and investors grew more concerned about the world's second-largest economy. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par The Scariest Place for Investors in a Trade War \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Many investors have already started selling Europe, concerned trade frictions between the U.S. and China could dent a fragile economic recovery in a region heavily exposed to international trade. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par U.S. Signals Sanctions for All Imports of Iranian Oil \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. threatened to slap sanctions on countries that don't cut oil imports from Iran to "zero" by Nov. 4, part of the Trump administration's push to further isolate Tehran both politically and economically, a senior State Department official said. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par China Says Closely Watching U.S. Plan to Limit Chinese Investment \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's Ministry of Commerce\f28 \uc2 \f2  said Wednesday that is closely watching the U.S.'s plan to restrict Chinese investment amid signs of intensifying trade tensions between the two countries.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 June 27, 2018 05:00 ET (09:00 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020180627ee6r000oj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
