\par\par \b \uc2 China's 'Big Mama' Steps In as Yuan Tumbles Further -- Update\b0\par\par\uc2 By Saumya Vaishampayan and Shen Hong \par \uc2 788 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Juni 2018\par 10:52\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  appeared to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the yuan's decline on Wednesday, traders said, after it had earlier set the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a six-month low against the U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 The yuan plunged as much as 0.9% against the dollar in mainland China during the Asian morning, with one dollar buying 6.6159 yuan. That put the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at its weakest level since Dec. 19 on an intraday basis, according to Wind Info.\par \par \uc2 At least one state-owned bank, which has traditionally acted as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's agent when it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in markets, sold large amounts of dollars after the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  breached the 6.60 level against the yuan, traders said.\par \par \uc2 "The kind of dollar selling from that bank was so aggressive that we knew instantly that it must be from the Big Mama," said a Shanghai-based senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at an Asian bank, referring to the \uc2 Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  's nickname among local traders.\par \par \uc2 The People's Bank of China\uc2  didn't immediately respond to a request for comment.\par \par \uc2 The yuan pared its losses in afternoon trading, with one dollar recently buying 6.5969 yuan, putting the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on track to slip 0.6% for the day.\par \par \uc2 Similar action took place in the offshore market, where one dollar bought 6.6010 yuan recently. There, the yuan was down 0.3% against the dollar on the day after falling as much as 0.5% earlier.\par \par \uc2 The Chinese authorities seem willing to let the yuan continue to weaken, but by also \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in markets they are sending a message to investors that they want to keep the situation under control, traders said.\par \par \uc2 "The speed [at which] the yuan fell earlier today was obviously considered too fast from the authorities' point of view," said a Shanghai-based head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at a domestic bank.\par \par \uc2 Before trading began on Wednesday, \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had set the dollar's reference rate at 6.5569 yuan, which meant the yuan was 0.6% lower than the day before--its biggest drop since Jan. 9, 2017. The so-called fix, which reflects the previous day's close and the movement of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  overnight, put the yuan at its weakest level since Dec. 25.\par \par \uc2 The weaker fix followed a slump in the yuan's value against the dollar on Tuesday. The yuan doesn't trade freely like the dollar or Japanese yen; instead, \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  allows the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pair to trade 2% above and below its reference rate each day.\par \par \uc2 While many \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have fallen against the dollar in the past two months, the yuan's drop has accelerated since trade threats between China and the U.S. escalated in the middle of June.\par \par \uc2 In a sign of Beijing's tolerance of a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders pointed to an article published in the state-run Securities Daily on Wednesday, in which the paper quoted analysts as defending the yuan's recent slide.\par \par \uc2 "Moderate (yuan) depreciation is reasonable. It can leave some room for China to respond to the trade situation, so it's not a bad thing," wrote the Securities Daily.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had exacerbated the yuan's swoon on Sunday when it announced steps to free up more than $100 billion for banks to boost lending. At the same time, the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  appears committed to tighten monetary policy further thanks to a strong economy, a divergence that should weigh on the yuan.\par \par \uc2 Still, Chinese policy makers don't want to let the yuan weaken too much in case it triggers another cycle of capital outflows, which took place after the yuan's 2015 devaluation.\par \par \uc2 "It's a delicate balance [for Chinese policy makers]...due to the rising downside risk domestically for economic growth and the trade tensions brewing on the external front," said \uc2 Aidan Yao\uc2 , a senior emerging Asia economist at AXA Investment Managers in Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 With its decline this week, the yuan has now lost 1.3% against the dollar this year, catching up with the euro's 2.8% drop and the Korean won's 4.5% pullback in the same period. Until Monday, the yuan had actually risen for the year.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Yao said that if the policy makers begin to worry about the scale of yuan depreciation from monetary easing, they could rely on other tools to boost growth, including infrastructure spending.\par \par \uc2 Write to Saumya Vaishampayan at saumya.vaishampayan@wsj.com and Shen Hong at hong.shen@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 June 27, 2018 04:52 ET (08:52 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020180627ee6r000j8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
