\par\par \b \uc2 China\u8217\'20\'19s \u8216\'20\'18Big Mama\u8217\'20\'19 Steps In as Yuan Tumbles Further\b0\par\par\uc2 977 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Juni 2018\par 06:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 As China faces off against the U.S. in a burgeoning trade dispute, the country\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is allowing the yuan to weaken against the dollar while also trying to prevent the kind of sharp devaluation that could have a destabilizing effect on its own economy.\par \par \uc2 That balancing act was on display Wednesday: The yuan fell to a more-than-six-month low against the dollar before the \uc2 People\u8217\'20\'19s Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  appeared to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the decline.\par \par \uc2 Allowing the yuan to fall, analysts say, is one way Beijing could try to counter growing pressure from the U.S. on the country\u8217\'20\'19s trade practices, with tariffs on billions of dollars worth of Chinese exports due to come into effect early next month. A lower yuan versus the dollar would make China\u8217\'20\'19s exports more competitive, offsetting some of the tariff effect.\par \par \uc2 However, a sudden yuan devaluation could prove dangerous for China. It would risk a destabilizing capital flight, as happened after Beijing last allowed a sudden drop in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in August 2015. It could also heighten tensions with the Trump administration: The U.S. president often criticized China\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies during the campaign.\par \par \uc2 So while board macroeconomic factors currently argue for a weaker yuan, Beijing is having to balance that with a desire to not let the decline run out of control.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s a delicate balance [for Chinese policy makers]\u8230\'20\'26due to the rising downside risk domestically for economic growth and the trade tensions brewing on the external front,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Aidan Yao\uc2 , a senior emerging Asia economist at AXA Investment Managers in Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 The yuan doesn\u8217\'20\'19t trade freely like the dollar or Japanese yen; instead, \uc2 China\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  allows the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pair to trade 2% above and below its reference rate each day. While many \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have fallen against the dollar in the past two months, the yuan\u8217\'20\'19s drop has accelerated since trade threats between China and the U.S. escalated in the middle of June.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, thee yuan dropped as much as 0.9% against the dollar in mainland China during the Asian morning, with one dollar buying 6.6159 yuan. That put the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at its weakest level since Dec. 19 on an intraday basis, according to Wind Info.\par \par \uc2 At least one state-owned bank, which has traditionally acted as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s agent when it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in markets, sold large amounts of dollars after the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  breached the 6.60 level against the yuan, traders said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe kind of dollar selling from that bank was so aggressive that we knew instantly that it must be from the Big Mama,\u8221\'20\'1D said a Shanghai-based senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at an Asian bank, referring to the \uc2 Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s nickname among local traders.\par \par \uc2 The People\u8217\'20\'19s Bank of China\uc2  didn\u8217\'20\'19t immediately respond to a request for comment.\par \par \uc2 The yuan pared its losses in afternoon trading, with one dollar recently buying 6.5969 yuan, putting the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on track to slip 0.6% for the day.\par \par \uc2 Similar action took place in the offshore market, where one dollar bought 6.6010 yuan recently. There, the yuan was down 0.3% against the dollar on the day after falling as much as 0.5% earlier.\par \par \uc2 The Chinese authorities seem willing to let the yuan continue to weaken. But by also \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in markets they are sending a message to investors that they want to keep the situation under control, traders said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe speed [at which] the yuan fell earlier today was obviously considered too fast from the authorities\u8217\'20\'19 point of view,\u8221\'20\'1D said a Shanghai-based head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at a domestic bank.\par \par \uc2 Before trading began on Wednesday, \uc2 China\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had set the dollar\u8217\'20\'19s reference rate at 6.5569 yuan, which meant the yuan was 0.6% lower than the day before\u8212\'20\'14its biggest drop since Jan. 9, 2017. The so-called fix, which reflects the previous day\u8217\'20\'19s close and the movement of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  overnight, put the yuan at its weakest level since Dec. 25.\par \par \uc2 The weaker fix followed a slump in the yuan\u8217\'20\'19s value against the dollar on Tuesday. In a sign of Beijing\u8217\'20\'19s tolerance of a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders pointed to an article published in the state-run Securities Daily on Wednesday, in which the paper quoted analysts as defending the yuan\u8217\'20\'19s recent slide.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CModerate (yuan) depreciation is reasonable. It can leave some room for China to respond to the trade situation, so it\u8217\'20\'19s not a bad thing,\u8221\'20\'1D wrote the Securities Daily.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had exacerbated the yuan\u8217\'20\'19s swoon on Sunday when it announced steps to free up more than $100 billion for banks to boost lending. At the same time, the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  appears committed to tighten monetary policy further thanks to a strong economy, a divergence that should weigh on the yuan.\par \par \uc2 Still, Chinese policy makers don\u8217\'20\'19t want to let the yuan weaken too much in case it triggers another cycle of capital outflows, which took place after the yuan\u8217\'20\'19s 2015 devaluation.\par \par \uc2 With its decline this week, the yuan has now lost 1.3% against the dollar this year, catching up with the euro\u8217\'20\'19s 2.8% drop and the Korean won\u8217\'20\'19s 4.5% pullback in the same period. Until Monday, the yuan had actually risen for the year.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Yao said that if the policy makers begin to worry about the scale of yuan depreciation from monetary easing, they could rely on other tools to boost growth, including infrastructure spending.\par \par \uc2 Write to Saumya Vaishampayan at saumya.vaishampayan@wsj.com and Shen Hong at hong.shen@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 June 27, 2018 00:00 ET (04:00 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020180627ee6r00023\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
