\par\par \b \uc2 EXPLAINER-China  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  moves hang in the balance in U.S. trade spat\b0\par\par\uc2 960 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 April 2018\par 20:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2018 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 April 17 (Reuters) - U.S. President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's latest complaint about China devaluing its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is off base. But the yuan could potentially become a major sticking point in efforts to resolve the trade dispute between the world's two biggest economies.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  valuations have long played a role in global trade spats because the goods of countries with weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have a price advantage over those sold from stronger-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  nations. China has weakened the yuan at times in the past but more recently it has engineered a strengthening. Retaliating against the Trump administration by weakening it any time soon is seen as a long shot.\par \par \uc2 Any attempt by China to engineer a weakening, or devaluation, of the yuan might only escalate trade tensions between Washington and Beijing, while providing little relief to Chinese exporters facing the onslaught of tariffs that Trump has imposed.\par \par \uc2 Devaluation is performed by a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  or through other official channels. This contrasts with depreciation of a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , where the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market sets its value.\par \par \uc2 Many analysts say China will be loath to let the yuan depreciate or use it as a tool in the trade war, and Chinese President \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2 's somewhat conciliatory speech last Tuesday also blunted speculation about yuan depreciation. Trump's tweet Monday risks inflaming bilateral tensions, however.\par \par \uc2 Q: Has China been manipulating its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 Not directly. Because the yuan is managed in a tight band around a daily reference rate, there is always the suspicion of official bias in its movement. But China allowed its yuan to rise 6.8 percent against the U.S. dollar in 2017. In recent months, it has kept the yuan nearly constant against a broad trade-weighted \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket. That makes it less volatile than most market-determined \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  systems, but \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not been seen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yuan or overtly prevent its appreciation.\par \par \uc2 Q: How might China weaken its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 A: Beijing could potentially lower its daily fixing of the yuan against the dollar. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves in a 2 percent band around the daily reference rate. Beijing might also signal to its state-owned and affiliated banks to sell yuan and to buy the dollar and Treasuries.\par \par \uc2 Alternatively, Beijing could re-introduce the counter-cyclical factor that helped the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  put a floor under the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2016 and 2017. It could also relax rules on retail and corporate capital outflows.\par \par \uc2 But so far this year, the yuan, or renminbi, has risen 3.2 percent against the dollar following a 6.8 percent increase in 2017, the biggest rise in nine years, according to Reuters data. The yuan has been mostly steady against its trade-weighted index for most of this year.\par \par \uc2 Of course, the market could suddenly sell the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on concerns about the impact of U.S. tariffs on China's exports or broader economic activity.\par \par \uc2 Q: Would this change China's holdings of U.S. Treasuries?\par \par \uc2 A: China is the biggest foreign holder of \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  debt at $1.177 trillion at the end of February. To weaken the yuan, the world's second-biggest economy could also use dollars to buy more Treasuries. That, in turn, would actually help the United States, since its borrowing needs are going up after a tax overhaul last year and the two-year federal spending deal in February.\par \par \uc2 Q: Why is a deliberate weakening of the yuan seen as unlikely?\par \par \uc2 A devaluation would give Washington an opportunity to label China a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator and to justify trade sanctions.\par \par \uc2 It could also hurt confidence in China's markets and spark potentially destabilizing capital outflows, as happened after a surprise 2 percent devaluation of the yuan in August 2015\par \par \uc2 Countries in East Asia might follow China's lead and devalue to prevent China from having too much of a competitive advantage, said \uc2 Marc Chandler\uc2 , global head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at BBH.\par \par \uc2 China's government has been focusing on reducing high levels of debt in the economy. If it were to undermine investor confidence in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it would complicate the deleveraging process.\par \par \uc2 Q: How might United States respond to a deliberate yuan weakening?\par \par \uc2 Embarking on a move to depreciate the yuan might rekindle the ire of Trump, who has branded the Chinese "world champions" of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.S. Treasury Department\uc2  under Trump has not yet labeled China as a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator.\par \par \uc2 Q: Is a weaker yuan an effective trade weapon?\par \par \uc2 A: While a lower yuan would make Chinese goods cheaper for U.S. consumers and importers in the long run, its trade impact would not felt immediately. A sustained weakening of the yuan could also make it harder for China to attract foreign investment.\par \par \uc2 Many U.S. importers, particularly smaller ones, have locked in the costs of their goods through longer-term contracts.\par \par \uc2 "I don't expect any quick impact," said Minh Trang, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at Silicon Valley Bank in Santa Clara, California.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. trade deficit with China contracted in February to $29.26 billion, but it still made up more than half of its overall trade deficit. The overall U.S. trade shortfall widened to $57.6 billion, the biggest monthly gap in 9-1/2 years.\par \par \uc2 What about equity markets?\par \par \uc2 If the reaction is similar to August 2015, when China unexpectedly devalued the yuan by about 2 percent, shares of companies with exposure to China could see their stocks hit, and there could be a broad-based sell-off. (With additional reporting by Vidya Ranganthan in Singapore and Megan Davies in New York; Editing by Tom Brown) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2018-4-17T19:15:46.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020180417ee4h00seb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
