\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Asia stocks edge up on steady China GDP, earnings in focus\b0\par\par\uc2 561 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 April 2018\par 05:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2018 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * MSCI Asia-Pacific index up 0.05 pct, Nikkei flat               \par \par \uc2 * China Q1 GDP up 6.8 pct, slightly stronger than forecast               \par \par \uc2 * But China industrial output misses forecast, Q1 investment slows               \par \par \uc2 * Pound rises to post-Brexit referendum high               \par \par \uc2 * Crude oil bounces modestly after overnight slide               \par \par \uc2 By Shinichi Saoshiro               \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, April 17 (Reuters) - Asia stocks rose modestly on Tuesday following data showing China's economy grew a little faster than expected in the first quarter.               \par \par \uc2 The dollar was barely changed, with demand for safe-haven U.S. Treasuries ebbing as investor risk appetite improved in parts of the broader markets as investors took the view Western-led strikes on Syria were a one-off \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .               \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economy grew a welcome 6.8 percent in the first quarter of 2018 from a year earlier, official data showed on Tuesday, unchanged from the previous quarter.               \par \par \uc2 But separate data showed March industrial output missed expectations and first-quarter fixed-asset investment growth slowed, tempering equity market gains.               \par \par \uc2 MSCI's broadest index of Asia-Pacific shares outside Japan edged up 0.05 percent.               \par \par \uc2 Australian stocks gained 0.5 percent, South Korea's KOSPI dipped 0.1 percent and Hong Kong's Hang Seng gained 0.15 percent.               \par \par \uc2 Shanghai rose  0.15 percent and Japan's Nikkei was flat.               \par \par \uc2 While Saturday's missile strikes were the biggest \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Western countries against Syria, investor risk appetite improved on speculation that the attacks would not lead to prolonged conflict.               \par \par \uc2 "The markets had been bracing for a possible escalation in Syria following President Trump's earlier warnings. Military action, however, has been limited, bringing relief," said Kota Hirayama, senior emerging markets economist at SMBC Nikko Securities in Tokyo.               \par \par \uc2 "That said, the underlying picture has not changed. Conflict continues in Syria and trade issues remain unresolved. Geopolitics will impact the markets again."               \par \par \uc2 The Dow gained 0.87 percent and the S&P 500 rose 0.8 percent on Monday, with the biggest boosts from technology and healthcare sectors as investors were optimistic about the earnings season and appeared less worried about U.S.-led missile attacks in Syria.               \par \par \uc2 S&P 500 companies are expected to report an 18.6 percent jump in first-quarter profit, on average, the biggest rise in seven years, according to \uc2 Thomson Reuters\uc2  data.               \par \par \uc2 The dollar index against a basket of six major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  was little changed at 89.441 after losing 0.4 percent overnight.               \par \par \uc2 The euro was steady at $1.2377. The dollar was effectively flat at 107.060 yen               \par \par \uc2 The pound rose to $1.4355, its highest since June 2016, with focus on data that could cement expectations of a May interest rate increase from the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2 .               \par \par \uc2 The Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  (HKMA) stepped into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market again on Tuesday, buying HK$5.77 billion ($735 million) in Hong Kong dollars as the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  repeatedly hit the lower end of its allowable trading band.               \par \par \uc2 The 10-year \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  note yield was at 2.834 percent after rising to 2.865 on Monday, its highest since March 22.               \par \par \uc2 U.S. crude oil futures rose 0.4 percent to $66.49 a barrel after tumbling nearly 1.8 percent overnight as concerns over tensions in the Middle East waned.               \par \par \uc2 Released: 2018-4-17T04:11:39.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020180417ee4h0058x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
