Ss issued by the \par      HKICPA. In addition, the consolidated financial statements include applicable disclosures required by \par      the Rules Governing the Listing of Securities on the Stock Exchange and by the Hong Kong Companies \par      Ordinance. \par  \par  \par      Basis of preparation \par  \par  \par      The consolidated financial statements have been prepared on the historical cost basis except for certain \par      financial instruments that are measured at fair values at the end of each reporting period, as explained in \par      the accounting policies set out below. \par  \par  \par      Historical cost is generally based on the fair value of the consideration given in exchange for goods and \par      services. \par  \par  \par      Fair value is the price that would be received to sell an asset or paid to transfer a liability in an orderly \par      transaction between market participants at the measurement date, regardless of whether that price is \par      directly observable or estimated using another valuation technique. In estimating the fair value of an asset \par      or a liability, the Group takes into account the characteristics of the asset or liability if market participants \par      would take those characteristics into account when pricing the asset or liability at the measurement \par      date. Fair value for measurement and/or disclosure purposes in these consolidated financial statements \par      is determined on such a basis, except for share-based payment transactions that are within the scope of \par      HKFRS 2 Share-based Payment, leasing transactions that are within the scope of HKAS 17 Leases, and \par      measurements that have some similarities to fair value but are not fair value, such as net realisable value in \par      HKAS 2 Inventories or value in use in HKAS 36 Impairment of Assets. \par  \par  \par      In addition, for financial reporting purposes, fair value measurements are categorised into Level 1, 2 or 3 \par      based on the degree to which the inputs to the fair value measurements are observable and the significance \par      of the inputs to the fair value measurement in its entirety, which are described as follows: \par  \par  \par            Level 1 inputs are quoted prices (unadjusted) in active markets for identical assets or liabilities that \par             the entity can access at the measurement date; \par  \par  \par            Level 2 inputs are inputs, other than quoted prices included within Level 1, that are observable for \par             the asset or liability, either directly or indirectly; and \par  \par  \par            Level 3 inputs are unobservable inputs for the asset or liability. \par  \par  \par  \par  \par                                                           7 \par The principal accounting policies are set out below. \par  \par  \par Basis of consolidation \par  \par  \par The consolidated financial statements incorporate the financial statements of the Company and entities \par (including structured entities) controlled by the Company and its subsidiaries. Control is achieved when \par the Company: \par  \par  \par       has power over the investee; \par  \par  \par       is exposed, or has rights, to variable returns from its involvement with the investee; and \par  \par  \par       has the ability to use its power to affect its returns. \par  \par  \par The Group reassesses whether or not it controls an investee if facts and circumstances indicate that there \par are changes to one or more of the three elements of control listed above. \par  \par  \par Consolidation of a subsidiary begins when the Group obtains control over the subsidiary and ceases when \par the Group loses control of the subsidiary. Specifically, income and expenses of a subsidiary acquired \par or disposed of during the year are included in the consolidated statement of profit or loss and other \par comprehensive income from the date the Group gains control until the date when the Group ceases to \par control the subsidiary. \par  \par  \par Profit or loss and each item of other comprehensive income are attributed to the owners of the Company \par and to the non-controlling interests. Total comprehensive income of subsidiaries is attributed to the owners \par of the Company and to the non-controlling interests even if this results in the non-controlling interests \par having a deficit balance. \par  \par  \par When necessary, adjustments are made to the financial statements of subsidiaries to bring their accounting \par policies into line with the Group's accounting policies. \par  \par  \par All intragroup assets and liabilities, equity, income, expenses and cash flows relating to transactions \par between members of the Group are eliminated in full on consolidation. \par  \par  \par  \par  \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 27 Mar 2018 06:54 ET \par HK Bourse: Results Announcement From \f28 \uc2 \f2 Shandong Weigao Group Medical Polymer Co. Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2  . -3-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par  \par Changes in the Group 's ownership interests in existing subsidiaries \par  \par  \par Changes in the Group's ownership interests in subsidiaries that do not result in the Group losing control \par over the subsidiaries are accounted for as equity transactions. The carrying amounts of the Group's \par relevant components of equity and the non-controlling interests are adjusted to reflect the changes in their \par relative interests in the subsidiaries, including re-attribution of relevant reserves between the Group and \par the non- controlling interests according to the Group's and the non-controlling interests' proportionate \par interests. \par  \par  \par  \par  \par                                                     8 \par      Any difference between the amount by which the non-controlling interests are adjusted, and the fair \par      value of the consideration paid or received is recognised directly in equity and attributed to owners of the \par      Company. \par  \par  \par      When the Group loses control of a subsidiary, the assets and liabilities of that subsidiary and non- \par      controlling interest (if any) are derecognised. A gain or loss is recognised in profit or loss and is calculated \par      as the difference between (i) the aggregate of the fair value of the consideration received and the fair \par      value of any retained interest and (ii) the previous carrying amount of the assets (including goodwill), \par      and liabilities attributable to the owners of the Company. All amounts previously recognised in other \par      comprehensive income in relation to that subsidiary are accounted for as if the Group had directly \par      disposed of the related assets or liabilities of the subsidiary (i.e. reclassified that to profit or loss or \par      transferred to another category of equity as specified/permitted by applicable HKFRSs). The fair value \par      of any investment retained in the former subsidiary at the date when control is lost is regarded as the fair \par      value on initial recognition for subsequent accounting under HKAS 39 or, when applicable, the cost on \par      initial recognition of an investment in an associate or a joint venture. \par  \par  \par 2.   REVENUE AND SEGMENT INFORMATION \par  \par  \par      The Group is principally engaged in the research and development, production and sale of single-use \par      medical device products, orthopaedic products, and operates finance lease and factoring business in the \par      PRC. \par  \par  \par      For management purposes, the Group is currently organised into three operating divisions - single use \par      medical device products, orthopaedic products, and others. These divisions are the basis of the internal \par      reports about components of the Group that are regularly reviewed by the chief operating decision maker \par      (Managing Director) in order to allocate resources to segments and to assess their performance. \par  \par  \par      Principal activities of the Group's operating segments are as follows: \par  \par  \par      Single use medical device products      -    production and sale of single use consumables such as infusion \par                                                      sets, syringes, blood transfusion sets and blood bags. \par      Orthopaedic products                    -    production and sale of orthopaedic products. \par      Others                                  -    finance lease and factoring business. \par  \par  \par      The operating segment regarding the manufacture and sale of blood purification products was discontinued \par      in the current year. The segment information reported on the next pages does not include any amounts for \par      this discontinued \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which are described in more details in note 6. \par  \par  \par  \par  \par                                                         9 \par Segment revenues and results \par  \par  \par The following is an analysis of the Group's revenue and results from continuing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by reportable \par segments: \par  \par  \par 2017 \par  \par  \par Continuing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par  \par                                         Single use \par                                           medical \par                                            device         Orthopaedic \par                                          products            products     Others   Eliminations       Total \par                                         RMB '000            RMB '000    RMB '000      RMB '000     RMB '000 \par  \par Revenue \par                                         5,422,255             870,386          -              -    6,292,641 \par   External sales \par       