\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-China  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  reserves fall in February, first drop in 13 months\b0\par\par\uc2 610 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2018\par 09:54\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2018 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     * China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves fall $27 bln in February\par     * Economists polled by Reuters expected reserves to drop by\par $1 bln\par     * Regulator says decline due to global \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rate\par fluctuations\par     * Rising U.S. rates could pressure yuan, revive outflow\par worries\par \par     BEIJING, March 7 (Reuters) - China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par reserves fell in February, posting their first decline in 13\par months, as the yuan weakened against the U.S. dollar amid wild\par swings in global financial markets.   \par     Reserves fell $27 billion to $3.134 trillion, compared with\par an increase of $21.5 billion in January, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  data\par showed on Wednesday. \par     Economists polled by Reuters had expected reserves to drop\par by $1 billion in February to $3.160 trillion.    \par     The \f28 \uc2 \f2 State Administration of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (SAFE) said\par adjustments in the value of China's holdings of non-dollar\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and assets led to the decline for the month, adding\par that the size of its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves will remain\par basically stable.\par     "Our model suggests that the \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  largely remained on the\par sidelines, with most of the decrease in the value of reserves\par due to exchange rate movements," \f28 \uc2 \f2 Capital Economics\f28 \uc2 \f2 ' Senior China\par Economist Julian Evans-Pritchard wrote in a note, referring to\par whether the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in markets last month. \par     Capital flight was seen as a major risk for China at the\par start of 2017, but a combination of tighter capital controls and\par a faltering dollar helped the yuan stage a strong turnaround,\par restoring confidence in the economy.\par     China's reserves rose for the first time last year since\par 2014. They had slumped nearly $1 trillion to $2.998 trillion by\par January 2017 as authorities sought to shore up the yuan and\par reduce potentially destabilising capital outflows. \par     Last year was a turning point as China's cross-border\par capital flows went from net outflows to stable, SAFE spokeswoman\par Wang Chunying told reporters in January.              \par     The yuan rose around 6.8 percent against the greenback in\par 2017, reversing three straight years of depreciation. \par     The gains stretched into this year, with January's 3.5\par percent rise the yuan's best monthly performance since 1994. But\par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  weakened 0.6 percent to the dollar in February in\par its first monthly drop since September.            \par     February's dollar gains were triggered by views that the\par U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could raise interest rates at a faster pace,\par but the greenback has wilted again this month as President\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Donald Trump\f28 \uc2 \f2 's plans to impose tariffs on imported steel and\par aluminium spark fears of a global trade war.            \par     "If concerns about U.S. protectionism continue to build, it\par will weaken global investors' appetite for emerging market\par assets," said Evans-Pritchard, who also argued that slower\par growth in China will likely trigger unexpected Chinese rate cuts\par this year. \par     "In both cases, capital outflows from \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will likely pick\par up. If the \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  does not \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , it would spark speculation\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  wants the yuan to weaken, which would revive outflows and\par depreciation pressure." \par     The \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  is widely expected to raise rates\par again on March 21, a day after China's annual parliament meeting\par is slated to end.\par     In response, most market watchers believe the \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  will make\par small adjustments to the rates it charges for short- and\par medium-term OMO loans, including 7-day reverse repurchase\par agreements.             \par     The value of China's gold reserves fell to $78.064 billion\par at the end of February, from $79.675 billion at end-January.\par \par     \par  (Reporting by Yawen Chen and Kevin Yao; Additional Reporting by\par Fang Cheng; Editing by Kim Coghill)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2018-3-7T09:54:34.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020180307ee3700bt9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
