\par\par \b \uc2 China's Deleveraging Efforts to Weigh on Growth in 2018 -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,284 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Februar 2018\par 05:50\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0450 GMT - China's gauges of manufacturing activity suggest some softness at the start of the year, as credit tightening starts to weigh on growth, says \uc2 Mizuho\uc2  economist \uc2 Shen Jianguang\uc2 . The continued deleveraging push will slow investment, though this will be offset by solid consumption and exports, he says. The slower growth of export orders, reflected in January's PMIs, will be temporary as the economic recoveries in the US and Europe shore up demand for Chinese goods, Shen says. Beijing will continue to reduce some government-led investment this year, he adds. (grace.zhu@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0447 GMT - Australia's Labor opposition faces a by-election test after one of its members became the latest lawmaker to be swept up by a citizenship crisis that last year saw the conservative government temporarily thrown into minority. David Feeney says he'll quit Parliament after finding he may hold UK citizenship in breach of constitutional rules barring dual citizens from office. Labor may struggle to win the subsequent election to replace him against Greens party opponents. Feeney is 1 of 4 opposition lawmakers with dual citizenship concerns. A loss would be a blow to center-left Labor's renewed momentum since policy missteps by Prime Minister \uc2 Malcolm Turnbull\uc2  and the start of the citizenship crisis. (david.winning@wsj.com; @dwinningWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0418 GMT - Following Taiwan's better-than-expected economic growth in 4Q and 2017, DBS raises its 2018 growth forecast to 2.8% from 2.5% and flags the risk of an earlier move by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise rates. The bank has been among the more optimistic on the island's economy and the latest data support its bullish view based on the island's high exposure to external trade amid a global recovery in 2017. Leading indicators, such as export orders, suggest that growth momentum will remain strong in 1Q, the bank says, increasing the chances that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will shift to tighten before the 4Q as currently forecast by DBS. (chester.yung@wsj.com;@chester_yung)\par \par \uc2 0246 GMT - Tighter liquidity in Singapore, with loan growth outpacing deposits, will allow banks to hike interest rates and improve margins, says \uc2 Nomura\uc2  after \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data on December lending. Local banks' loans-to-deposits ratio rose to 107.5% from 103.5% at the end of 2016, with the rate for foreign banks up to 99.8% from 95.3%. As the ratios rise, so will net interest margins, says \uc2 Nomura\uc2 . Singapore's 3 majors have all pushed up fixed-rate mortgages despite a dip in local benchmark rates. Loans by domestic banks rose 5.6% last year, and foreign-bank lending growth was double that. Both groups logged their biggest growth since 2014. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0244 GMT - A flat reading in Caixin's manufacturing \uc2 PMI\uc2  hints that the demand is weaker, says Jualian Evans Pritchard at \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  . That after the official manufacturing \uc2 PMI\uc2  out yesterday hit an 8-month low for January. CapEcon tends to put more weight on the Caixin PMI and says the data report suggests growth momentum was broadly steady. Still, "looking ahead we expect demand to soften further over the course of this year." (grace.zhu@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0132 GMT - There's no sign that Australia is about to break out of its low-inflation trap, says \uc2 Westpac\uc2  economist Justin Smirk, with yesterday's 4Q CPI continuing a run of soft price prints. Even with inflationary expectations drifting back towards the long-run average--\uc2 Westpac\uc2  suspects that rising power bills are responsible for that--there is little to suggest that the Australian economy is about to escape low inflation overall. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0124 GMT - China guides the yuan 0.5% stronger against the US dollar Thursday, fixing the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at its highest level since August 2015. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sets the dollar's reference rate at CNY6.3045, compared with CNY6.3339 on Wednesday. The stronger yuan fixing follows a surge in the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currencythe\b0 \highlight\uc2  previous day, with one dollar buying CNY 6.2920 at the close of trading. It's notable that while the yuan strengthened 0.8% against the dollar Wednesday, that was only its biggest jump in less than a week, underscoring how \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swings have picked up around the world. (saumya.vaishampayan@wsj.com; @saumvaish)\par \par \uc2 0116 GMT - Keep a possible 5% fall in Sydney and Melbourne prices in 2018 in perspective, says \uc2 Craig James\uc2  , chief economist at Commsec. The rebalancing of supply and demand comes after prices in Melbourne and Sydney gained around 90% in the past 9 years. That should still give home buyers plenty of reason to be positive. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @jamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0110 GMT - As chatter about the \uc2 PBoC\uc2  possibly stepping in to cool the yuan swirls, ING greater \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist \uc2 Iris Pang\uc2  doesn't see \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  happening. Dollar-yuan has been hit in part by Chinese exporters selling greenbacks ahead of Chinese New Year, she notes. But last month's 3% gain is still cause for pause, especially those who need to exchange dollars for yuan. Even if folks think the yuan may rise further, Pang isn't convinced it's enough to force the \uc2 PBoC\uc2 's hand as that may run counter to the optics of liberalizing the exchange market. She thinks \uc2 PBoC\uc2  may limit "hot money" coming into China, primarily in its bond markets, if the dollar hits CNY6.2 this week. It's around CNY6.29. (kenan.machado@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0103 GMT - Australian building approvals dropped 20% in December from November, with apartments plunging close to 40%. But that after growth through November was 13%, so the sequential slide probably isn't as alarming as it appears. In trend terms, approvals were down just 1.7%, so that should restore some sanity to the data set. And Australia is still building a lot of apartments while large projects can come in or go out of the data. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0054 GMT - Speculators potential putting "significant" pressure on dollar-yuan could prompt the \uc2 PBoC\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says Mark Williams, chief Asia economist at \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2 . The yuan's jumped 0.7% onshore yesterday versus the dollar to CNY6.2920. The pair was around CNY6.20 for months before the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's August 2015 devaluation of the yuan. But Williams also notes the yuan's gains being driven by the dollar's weakness, not big, broad gains versus a host of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . He says the \uc2 PBoC\uc2  could relax controls on outward capital flows or directly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market. (kenan.machado@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2310 GMT - At a CFR event in New York, Canadian Foreign Minister \uc2 Chrystia Freeland\uc2  was pressed by audience members on what is the country's "red-line" Nafta issues. She mentioned 2, the first being the US demand on autos to have more regional content, including a minimum level for the US. Canada offered an informal proposal that broadens the definition of what counts as car content, but US Trade Representative \uc2 Robert Lighthizer\uc2  dismissed the idea this week, warning it could lead to job losses on the continent. Freeland also cited Nafta's Chapter 19 provision, which lets expert, binational panels settle disputes on tariffs between Nafta parties. "It was probably from the Canadian perspective the single biggest thing the original" created, she said, adding Canada could be open to modifying or improving the process. (paul.vieira@wsj.com; @paulvieira)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 31, 2018 23:50 ET (04:50 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020180201ee21000er\par }\par \b \uc2 Zusammenfassung der Suche\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Text\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\uc2 (foreign exchange or fx or forex or currenc*) and (interven* or operation*) and (china near10 interven*) and (rst=trtw or rst=tprw or rst=tdjw)and (central bank or ministry of finance or treasury ministry or monetary authority)\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 {  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\row } \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Datum\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\uc2 01/01/1995 bis 21/08/2023\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\r